WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.029 --> 00:00:18.340
So the last time we talked, I talked to you
about how the knowledge structures that we

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:20.930
inhabit might be built from the bottom up.

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:27.650
So I’m showing you this diagram because
it might clarify that a little bit.

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:34.719
What you see at the top - this is a hierarchy,
a partially built hierarchy and so one way

00:00:34.719 --> 00:00:40.129
of thinking of an attribute like good when
you apply it to a person, or bad or evil or

00:00:40.129 --> 00:00:44.570
wonderful, one way to think about it is as
a description of an object, but it’s not

00:00:44.570 --> 00:00:45.570
really.

00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:51.120
It’s shorthand for a set of - well I think
of them as micro personalities, but you can

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:54.320
also think about them as behavioral patterns.

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:59.499
Now, one of the things I mentioned to you
about - I talked to you a little bit about

00:00:59.499 --> 00:01:05.920
words at one point, and what words signify
and this is a very complicated problem so

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:11.140
most of you are probably Augustinians without
even knowing it because St. Augustine originally

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:14.150
proposed that a word was a label for a thing.

00:01:14.150 --> 00:01:17.270
Of course, that’s how people naturally think.

00:01:17.270 --> 00:01:24.619
I’m sure most of you would regard that definition
as perfectly suitable, but it’s by no means

00:01:24.619 --> 00:01:30.750
the only definition and it’s by no means
necessarily the correct definition.

00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:34.750
There's a famous philosopher named Wittgenstein
who proposed instead that a word was like

00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:41.619
a piece in a chess game which meant that it
was something that you did something with,

00:01:41.619 --> 00:01:43.689
or you might think about it actually as a
tool.

00:01:43.689 --> 00:01:48.040
And when you think about how you use words,
you can see that you're using them often as

00:01:48.040 --> 00:01:57.320
tools to interact with other people for a
purpose.

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:03.670
What I like about the tool idea - the tool
idea is a very evolutionary sophisticated

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:10.979
concept, partly because we’re tool using
creatures probably beyond our other capacities

00:02:10.979 --> 00:02:17.090
except for possibly imitation and language
which set us so far apart from other animals,

00:02:17.090 --> 00:02:22.420
but the thing about a tool is that the difference
between a tool and an object doesn't have

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:23.420
a user.

00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:28.800
The word object doesn't imply user, but the
word tool implies user, and it’s not obvious

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:32.431
that we ever interact with anything in the
world without interacting with it in some

00:02:32.431 --> 00:02:34.110
motivated way.

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:37.549
And so, what we’re doing is we’re using
words to get through the world.

00:02:37.549 --> 00:02:42.010
To interact properly within the world and
to move where we want to go.

00:02:42.010 --> 00:02:44.200
So that’s the second way of thinking about
a word.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:46.790
Here’s another way of thinking about a word.

00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:52.670
A word is like a box of things and so imagine
that you cleaned out your basement and there's

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:56.750
a bunch of boxes downstairs and one box is
labeled books.

00:02:56.750 --> 00:03:00.940
Well inside that box, there could be any manner
of books.

00:03:00.940 --> 00:03:06.989
Books is a very very diverse set of phenomena.

00:03:06.989 --> 00:03:11.489
If you're moving, books are kind of condensed
into one thing which are kind of heavy objects

00:03:11.489 --> 00:03:14.690
made out of paper.

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:17.610
You can also imagine another box that says
things.

00:03:17.610 --> 00:03:21.519
And in the thing box, I’m sure you have
a thing drawer at home where you put all the

00:03:21.519 --> 00:03:25.970
things you don't know what to do with so it’s
like the category of all things you don't

00:03:25.970 --> 00:03:27.540
know what to do with.

00:03:27.540 --> 00:03:28.540
Things.

00:03:28.540 --> 00:03:36.920
Okay so a word can also represent a relatively
heterogenous set of tools or entities.

00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:43.409
That’s very very very useful to know because
one of the things that happens often when

00:03:43.409 --> 00:03:48.349
people are arguing is that they use the same
word but each word contains a set of different

00:03:48.349 --> 00:03:49.519
objects.

00:03:49.519 --> 00:03:54.939
You might say for example, is fear real?

00:03:54.939 --> 00:03:56.599
Questions like that are very strange.

00:03:56.599 --> 00:04:00.299
They're not really answerable in some sense
because it depends on exactly what you mean

00:04:00.299 --> 00:04:05.960
by fear because fear can contain a lot of
heterogenous entities, roughly speaking, and

00:04:05.960 --> 00:04:09.659
it means - it also depends on what you mean
by real.

00:04:09.659 --> 00:04:13.730
So you can define fear and you can define
real and so that fear is a subcategory of

00:04:13.730 --> 00:04:19.120
real or you can define fear and you can define
fear so that fear isn’t a subset of real.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:26.220
So, the idea that fear is a box that only
contains a certain set of entities or a certain

00:04:26.220 --> 00:04:31.100
set of tools and that reality is the same
is wrong, and people argue about those sorts

00:04:31.100 --> 00:04:34.680
of things all the time, and they make these
binary questions.

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:35.979
Is X a Y?

00:04:35.979 --> 00:04:41.610
Well, X and Y are generally not well enough
to find so that you can easily find an answer

00:04:41.610 --> 00:04:43.430
to a binary question like that.

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:49.250
Okay so, words can also represent a relatively
heterogenous set of attributes or objects

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:50.370
or tools.

00:04:50.370 --> 00:04:55.130
When you say that someone’s a good person
for example, you don’t mean they’re a

00:04:55.130 --> 00:05:01.350
good person all the time or that everything
they’ve ever done is good because they wouldn't

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:06.060
be a good person then, they would be a saint
or some other fictional character like that.

00:05:06.060 --> 00:05:11.410
So, when you’re applying the label - we’ll
call it label for the purposes of this argument.

00:05:11.410 --> 00:05:14.020
It’s a descriptive tool actually.

00:05:14.020 --> 00:05:18.870
When you’re applying a term like that to
someone, it’s a shorthand for a variety

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:21.699
of states that this person could exist in.

00:05:21.699 --> 00:05:24.910
The majority of which you regard as positive.

00:05:24.910 --> 00:05:28.970
You might say he’s a better person than
she is, and then you’d say well the proportion

00:05:28.970 --> 00:05:35.610
of attributes that she has - I think I said
she, whatever - it exceeds the proportion

00:05:35.610 --> 00:05:38.360
of positives that this other person has.

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:42.240
So the other way you can think about a word
is - this is more complicated, it involves

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:43.500
a little bit of visualization.

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:46.810
So imagine you have a child’s train on a
track.

00:05:46.810 --> 00:05:48.750
It’s going around and around the track.

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:53.190
And it’s got cars on it that you can put
things in, and all the cars are full of things,

00:05:53.190 --> 00:05:58.470
and then on the track there’s a box with
cutouts in it so that the train can pass through,

00:05:58.470 --> 00:06:01.160
and on top of the box is a word.

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:05.090
And the word is things, and the train is going
through the box.

00:06:05.090 --> 00:06:09.920
In some sense, what that would mean - imagine
the train is long enough - at any given second,

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:14.319
the box of things would contain different
things, but it would still be a box of things.

00:06:14.319 --> 00:06:16.889
And a lot of words we use are like that.

00:06:16.889 --> 00:06:24.259
Not only do they represent a heterogenous
set of entities, but the set changes depending

00:06:24.259 --> 00:06:27.740
on the context that we use the word in.

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:32.520
So the reason I’m telling you that is because
a word, here’s another way of thinking about

00:06:32.520 --> 00:06:33.520
a word.

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:39.620
This will be good for you computer - well
you’re all computer people now of course.

00:06:39.620 --> 00:06:43.710
A word is a low resolution image.

00:06:43.710 --> 00:06:47.280
So you think about it.

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:50.870
A word is a good enough approximation of the
thing that it’s representing.

00:06:50.870 --> 00:06:52.050
Sort of like a thumbnail.

00:06:52.050 --> 00:06:56.860
Now obviously one of the differences of a
thumbnail and the real thing is that the real

00:06:56.860 --> 00:07:01.910
thing exists at a much more, much higher level
of resolution.

00:07:01.910 --> 00:07:03.870
It exists at many, many more layers.

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:06.160
You can use the thumbnail to represent it.

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:11.550
The advantage to doing that is that it doesn’t
take up much processing time, and it doesn’t

00:07:11.550 --> 00:07:12.680
take up much memory.

00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:16.520
So lots of time, you want to use the shorthand
for the thing rather than the thing because

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:18.620
it’s just too complicated otherwise.

00:07:18.620 --> 00:07:25.009
And then you might say well how do you know
your shorthand representation is a good representation

00:07:25.009 --> 00:07:29.581
of the thing you're trying to represent, and
the pragmatists would say well it depends

00:07:29.581 --> 00:07:31.419
on what you want to do with it.

00:07:31.419 --> 00:07:33.050
That’s the tool-like aspect.

00:07:33.050 --> 00:07:40.360
It’s like if you’re using word X to represent
potentially heterogeneous class Y, and when

00:07:40.360 --> 00:07:44.099
you use it, you get to where you're want or
you get close enough to where you want, then

00:07:44.099 --> 00:07:45.639
it’s good enough.

00:07:45.639 --> 00:07:49.729
So it’s like how you know a joke is good
enough, well someone laughs.

00:07:49.729 --> 00:07:55.319
Okay, the joke is good enough, and so one
of the things that’s quite interesting about

00:07:55.319 --> 00:07:59.220
the way you interact with the world, and this
is something the pragmatists really discovered

00:07:59.220 --> 00:08:05.229
in some sense, was that you're always aiming
at some bounded set of events that’s somewhat

00:08:05.229 --> 00:08:09.490
different than the current set of events because
otherwise you could just sit there, and then

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:14.539
you put your conceptions to work and your
actions to work to manage the transformation,

00:08:14.539 --> 00:08:19.030
and if you manage the transformation, then
it’s good enough.

00:08:19.030 --> 00:08:21.490
You don’t have to go any farther than that.

00:08:21.490 --> 00:08:26.360
So that explains partly how you can maneuver
in the world with limited knowledge.

00:08:26.360 --> 00:08:33.500
You use low resolution representations to
partially hit vague goals, and that generally

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:34.500
gets you there.

00:08:34.500 --> 00:08:41.270
That process was called satisficing by - now
what’s his name, an extraordinarily famous

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:43.600
economist and psychologist.

00:08:43.600 --> 00:08:45.680
Anyone remember who coined the word satisficing?

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:48.810
Well, an economist psychologist would be Herb
Simon.

00:08:48.810 --> 00:08:50.380
That’s right, it’s Simon.

00:08:50.380 --> 00:08:54.970
It was Herb Simon, and you know he said well,
you’re often not out to optimize.

00:08:54.970 --> 00:08:59.390
Let’s say in a search for a partner, to
optimize it you’d have to search through

00:08:59.390 --> 00:09:00.720
several billion people.

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:04.170
Well you’re not gonna do that because you
don’t have the time.

00:09:04.170 --> 00:09:08.541
So then you might say well, you don’t know
exactly how to do it but it’s something

00:09:08.541 --> 00:09:09.970
this.

00:09:09.970 --> 00:09:13.380
You have five attributes that you’re looking
for and maybe two that you don’t know you’re

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:17.410
looking for but you are looking for, and the
first person that comes along that matches

00:09:17.410 --> 00:09:20.020
those well enough, you pick.

00:09:20.020 --> 00:09:25.660
So it’s not an optimization issue, but given
that you have a limited amount of time, you

00:09:25.660 --> 00:09:27.330
can’t wait around for optimized solutions.

00:09:27.330 --> 00:09:33.200
You have to go ahead with sufficient, and
then move to the next issue.

00:09:33.200 --> 00:09:41.390
Okay, so all that’s a conceptual precursor
for taking a look at this diagram.

00:09:41.390 --> 00:09:43.340
So we’re calling someone a good person.

00:09:43.340 --> 00:09:44.340
So what does it mean?

00:09:44.340 --> 00:09:45.930
Well we’re going to do a bit of a microanalysis.

00:09:45.930 --> 00:09:49.580
We’re going to assume it’s a bit of a
shorthand, low resolution representation of

00:09:49.580 --> 00:09:52.630
the person and that you can decompose it.

00:09:52.630 --> 00:09:57.310
If someone says X is a - Henry is a good person,
and maybe he’s 45.

00:09:57.310 --> 00:09:59.420
You might say does he have children?

00:09:59.420 --> 00:10:00.430
Then you might say yes.

00:10:00.430 --> 00:10:03.520
Then you might say well is he a good parent
because you might assume that if he’s a

00:10:03.520 --> 00:10:05.690
good person, he might be a good parent.

00:10:05.690 --> 00:10:10.030
Now it’s conceivable that he can be a good
parent without being a good parent if he was

00:10:10.030 --> 00:10:12.630
good at five other important things.

00:10:12.630 --> 00:10:16.440
But let’s assume as a good person he’s
also a good parent.

00:10:16.440 --> 00:10:21.130
I think it’s reasonable to assume that good
parent would be a subset of good person, right,

00:10:21.130 --> 00:10:23.130
because it’s more specific.

00:10:23.130 --> 00:10:28.920
More specific means it covers less time and
space in some sense because good person is

00:10:28.920 --> 00:10:33.140
a description of a person that covers how
the person interacts with the world across

00:10:33.140 --> 00:10:36.760
the broadest possible amount of time and location.

00:10:36.760 --> 00:10:41.200
And then good parent, well you can find that
out when they’re interacting with children

00:10:41.200 --> 00:10:42.850
let’s say for the sake of argument.

00:10:42.850 --> 00:10:46.750
There’s other issues, and then you might
say well if he’s a good parent what does

00:10:46.750 --> 00:10:49.660
he have to do to be a good parent?

00:10:49.660 --> 00:10:53.670
Well he has to feed his children so he probably
needs a job, and it would be better if it

00:10:53.670 --> 00:10:54.670
was a good job.

00:10:54.670 --> 00:10:58.560
So he’s got a good job and that’s part
of being a good person.

00:10:58.560 --> 00:11:05.810
And then maybe he can care for his family
and so care might be regarded as a more specific

00:11:05.810 --> 00:11:11.980
subset of good parent, and so you can decompose
caring to, let’s say, play with baby, and

00:11:11.980 --> 00:11:17.940
then you can decompose play into - well I
decomposed it into peek, tickle, and clean.

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:22.450
What’s interesting about this is those are
all abstractions, but once you get down to

00:11:22.450 --> 00:11:26.680
a high enough level of resolution, they’re
no longer abstractions in that they’re actually

00:11:26.680 --> 00:11:28.690
actions you undertake in the world.

00:11:28.690 --> 00:11:31.330
Moving your hand is not an abstraction.

00:11:31.330 --> 00:11:35.750
So at some point - and this is a nice elegant
solution to the mind body problem to some

00:11:35.750 --> 00:11:36.750
degree.

00:11:36.750 --> 00:11:41.930
At some level of resolution - high resolution
- it stops being conceptual and starts being

00:11:41.930 --> 00:11:45.250
action, and that's where the mind hits the
world.

00:11:45.250 --> 00:11:50.190
If you go into an even higher level of resolution
than that, like what do you have to do to

00:11:50.190 --> 00:11:51.350
move your hand?

00:11:51.350 --> 00:11:53.730
The answer to that is you don’t know.

00:11:53.730 --> 00:11:54.740
You just do it.

00:11:54.740 --> 00:11:59.800
And so your consciousness bottoms out, in
some sense, at the level of motor activity.

00:11:59.800 --> 00:12:04.420
So there’s a level above which you can’t
perceive which are the networks that you're

00:12:04.420 --> 00:12:10.400
involved in and there’s a level below which
you can’t perceive, which is under normal

00:12:10.400 --> 00:12:16.150
circumstances, procedural control which is
whatever enables you to actually move.

00:12:16.150 --> 00:12:21.130
So then you might say well what are all of
these things.

00:12:21.130 --> 00:12:24.130
How would you conceptualize a decomposed good
person.

00:12:24.130 --> 00:12:27.400
Well then you might say well what are all
these things made out of?

00:12:27.400 --> 00:12:29.550
In a sense, what are their constituent elements?

00:12:29.550 --> 00:12:33.050
Good person, good parent, family care, play
with baby.

00:12:33.050 --> 00:12:34.240
They're sort of like games.

00:12:34.240 --> 00:12:35.370
They're sort of like stories.

00:12:35.370 --> 00:12:37.170
They're sort of like rituals.

00:12:37.170 --> 00:12:43.370
I think about them in these terms, so if you
look at - that’s where we get to this particular

00:12:43.370 --> 00:12:47.760
diagram which we’re gonna concentrate on
quite a bit.

00:12:47.760 --> 00:13:11.130
Let me shift to it and we’ll talk about
that.

00:13:11.130 --> 00:13:28.040
Now I don’t exactly know how to - what to
call this thing.

00:13:28.040 --> 00:13:34.170
I think the best way of thinking about it
is it’s a subpersonality and that the reason

00:13:34.170 --> 00:13:38.070
I call it a subpersonality is because only
a personality has all the attributes that

00:13:38.070 --> 00:13:40.960
this thing has and so the attributes are.

00:13:40.960 --> 00:13:45.680
I talked to you last week about the fact that
the Russian neuropsychologist for example

00:13:45.680 --> 00:13:50.550
felt that people had to build models of the
world and so you can call this a model of

00:13:50.550 --> 00:13:52.820
the world.

00:13:52.820 --> 00:13:58.240
So the reason they suspected that was because
if you brought someone - I think I ran through

00:13:58.240 --> 00:14:02.190
this - if you bring someone into a laboratory
and you play them a tone, when they first

00:14:02.190 --> 00:14:07.520
hear the tone they manifest an orienting reflex
which is a change in skin conduction, and

00:14:07.520 --> 00:14:10.500
that’s actually a consequence of neurological
activity.

00:14:10.500 --> 00:14:14.290
And then if you play the tone a bunch, they
stop having the reaction and then if you vary

00:14:14.290 --> 00:14:23.130
the tone on any perceptible dimension, the
orienting reflex returns, and so the inference

00:14:23.130 --> 00:14:31.390
from that is obviously the person must be
constructing a model of the tone because otherwise

00:14:31.390 --> 00:14:35.620
how would they know if something deviated
from it, especially on all those dimensions.

00:14:35.620 --> 00:14:40.780
So not only is it a model, it’s a multi-dimensional
model.

00:14:40.780 --> 00:14:45.890
Okay, now the upshot of that is for a long
time, people tried to figure out how in the

00:14:45.890 --> 00:14:49.750
world we model the world, and then they tried
to figure out how to make smart machines that

00:14:49.750 --> 00:14:54.660
modeled the world that could then plan their
actions in the modeled world and then could

00:14:54.660 --> 00:14:56.290
act in the world as a consequence.

00:14:56.290 --> 00:15:00.930
They got nowhere with that because it turned
out that the world is so damn complex that

00:15:00.930 --> 00:15:04.020
if you’re gonna model it, you’re gonna
spend all the time modeling.

00:15:04.020 --> 00:15:07.240
You’re not gonna spend your time acting
in it and you’re not gonna get anywhere.

00:15:07.240 --> 00:15:11.930
It’s partly because the world is almost
infinitely complex, and so if you're trying

00:15:11.930 --> 00:15:16.060
to get a computer to look at the world, you
might say well what is the computer supposed

00:15:16.060 --> 00:15:17.240
to look at?

00:15:17.240 --> 00:15:18.240
Everything.

00:15:18.240 --> 00:15:19.750
Well, no.

00:15:19.750 --> 00:15:25.910
I mean, you look at a lot in some ways, and
not very much in others, and half your brain

00:15:25.910 --> 00:15:28.840
is dedicated, roughly speaking, to visual
processing.

00:15:28.840 --> 00:15:32.260
Then you’ve got your other senses to help
you figure out what’s going on.

00:15:32.260 --> 00:15:34.540
But you don’t build a model of the whole
world.

00:15:34.540 --> 00:15:37.540
You don’t build any model at all of the
parts of the world that aren’t relevant

00:15:37.540 --> 00:15:38.560
to you.

00:15:38.560 --> 00:15:42.560
For example, your model of this floor is very
low resolution.

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:47.270
If I took you guys out in the hallway, assuming
the hallway floor isn't the same color and

00:15:47.270 --> 00:15:49.450
I said, what was the color of this floor.

00:15:49.450 --> 00:15:52.620
You would not be able to tell me, and the
reason for that is who cares.

00:15:52.620 --> 00:15:57.260
It could be shifting color all the time while
you’re sitting here, you know, subtly, and

00:15:57.260 --> 00:15:58.260
you’re not gonna care.

00:15:58.260 --> 00:16:01.670
The reason for that is you’re not falling
through it, and it isn’t moving.

00:16:01.670 --> 00:16:05.930
So it’s like an invisible presupposition
of your current knowledge structure.

00:16:05.930 --> 00:16:08.900
And there's all sorts of things going on around
you that are like that.

00:16:08.900 --> 00:16:10.660
You don't pay attention to the walls.

00:16:10.660 --> 00:16:13.660
You really don't pay attention to the people
that are around you.

00:16:13.660 --> 00:16:16.210
They could sort themselves in a different
way.

00:16:16.210 --> 00:16:18.530
You wouldn't notice unless you know the person.

00:16:18.530 --> 00:16:21.520
There's also all sorts of things that you
don't model.

00:16:21.520 --> 00:16:25.460
Now you’ve all seen the invisible gorilla
video, I presume.

00:16:25.460 --> 00:16:26.920
Everyone’s seen that.

00:16:26.920 --> 00:16:30.470
Is there anyone here who hasn't seen that
video.

00:16:30.470 --> 00:16:31.470
Okay good.

00:16:31.470 --> 00:16:32.470
So that’s something eh.

00:16:32.470 --> 00:16:35.700
It’s really something that there's a psychology
experiment that's so well known you can talk

00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:37.910
to a crowd now and almost everyone’s seen
it.

00:16:37.910 --> 00:16:38.960
It’s so shocking.

00:16:38.960 --> 00:16:42.560
It’s like, of course I can see a gorilla
if one was dancing around on a basketball

00:16:42.560 --> 00:16:43.560
court.

00:16:43.560 --> 00:16:45.030
It’s like, ha no you wouldn’t.

00:16:45.030 --> 00:16:47.410
Or there's a fifty percent chance you wouldn’t.

00:16:47.410 --> 00:16:54.490
Now the reason for that is, fundamentally
speaking, is you're given a task when you

00:16:54.490 --> 00:16:59.330
watch that video, and the task is to count
the number of times the ball is thrown by

00:16:59.330 --> 00:17:01.870
the team in black.

00:17:01.870 --> 00:17:06.920
All of a sudden, your perceptual systems reorient
themselves and you're not really looking at

00:17:06.920 --> 00:17:07.920
people.

00:17:07.920 --> 00:17:13.760
You’re looking at something that might be
animated stick figures who are engaged, momentarily,

00:17:13.760 --> 00:17:19.020
in moving the object of perceptual importance
around.

00:17:19.020 --> 00:17:22.770
So you can blur them out, which you do because
obviously you can’t tell the difference

00:17:22.770 --> 00:17:26.160
between a gorilla and the person dressed in
black.

00:17:26.160 --> 00:17:28.430
The reason you can’t is you don't need to.

00:17:28.430 --> 00:17:35.020
In order to fulfill the task, you can treat
all the people like vaguely moving black objects,

00:17:35.020 --> 00:17:39.400
and as long as that doesn't interfere with
your counting, then it’s irrelevant.

00:17:39.400 --> 00:17:43.070
So actually, you don’t build a model of
the whole world.

00:17:43.070 --> 00:17:47.730
And you build a model of only the parts of
the world that are relevant to your ongoing

00:17:47.730 --> 00:17:48.730
micro action.

00:17:48.730 --> 00:17:53.060
Now the problem that brings up is how do you
know what’s relevant.

00:17:53.060 --> 00:17:55.640
Well that’s a tough question.

00:17:55.640 --> 00:18:01.010
I would say, the reason you know what’s
relevant is because some things don’t change

00:18:01.010 --> 00:18:05.650
over time, and you’ve been evolving through
three and a half billion years.

00:18:05.650 --> 00:18:08.150
So you’ve built yourself.

00:18:08.150 --> 00:18:13.300
If we go back to that other diagram, you can
imagine the levels there from the micro activities

00:18:13.300 --> 00:18:15.320
to the abstractions.

00:18:15.320 --> 00:18:19.660
You think about how much building had to go
in so you could undertake those microactions.

00:18:19.660 --> 00:18:21.650
So like, here you can move your hand.

00:18:21.650 --> 00:18:23.220
Well that’s great.

00:18:23.220 --> 00:18:26.020
How do you get the hand?

00:18:26.020 --> 00:18:28.260
Well, you know, it took a long time.

00:18:28.260 --> 00:18:32.510
It took almost all of the three and a half
billion years before you managed to come up

00:18:32.510 --> 00:18:34.000
with a hand.

00:18:34.000 --> 00:18:41.120
So, even underneath the microactions, there's
this massive evolutionary history that's been

00:18:41.120 --> 00:18:45.780
built up partially as a consequence of some
of the unchanging features of the environment,

00:18:45.780 --> 00:18:50.700
or at least in consequence to some of the
slower changing features of the environment

00:18:50.700 --> 00:18:55.170
that has given you this incredibly complex
body that’s really good at doing some things

00:18:55.170 --> 00:18:57.800
and not really good at doing other things
at all.

00:18:57.800 --> 00:19:01.940
And so, that’s how you solve the relevance
problem, and I actually think it’s one of

00:19:01.940 --> 00:19:07.170
the most powerful arguments for the necessity
of evolutionary theory.

00:19:07.170 --> 00:19:11.590
It’s like this problem is so complex because
it’s not only how you have to adapt to the

00:19:11.590 --> 00:19:13.070
environment across time.

00:19:13.070 --> 00:19:15.380
So you have to build a body that adapts to
the environment.

00:19:15.380 --> 00:19:16.620
It’s worse than that.

00:19:16.620 --> 00:19:19.340
It’s like the train passing through the
box.

00:19:19.340 --> 00:19:20.840
The environment is not stable.

00:19:20.840 --> 00:19:25.550
It keeps shifting and turning and moving and
twisting, and so not only do you have to adapt

00:19:25.550 --> 00:19:35.090
to the environment, you have to adapt flexibly
to the environment while it transforms itself

00:19:35.090 --> 00:19:40.570
at different levels of totality because sometimes
there’s minor changes to the environment.

00:19:40.570 --> 00:19:45.050
Sometimes a meteor hits the Earth and we’re
plunged into darkness for four years, or there’s

00:19:45.050 --> 00:19:46.670
an ice age or something like that.

00:19:46.670 --> 00:19:52.120
So the levels of transformation in the environment
can be radical and the evolutionary process

00:19:52.120 --> 00:19:53.780
has to keep up with that.

00:19:53.780 --> 00:19:57.670
The way it works, apparently, like I don’t
believe this is the whole story although it’s

00:19:57.670 --> 00:20:02.840
more or less the whole Darwinian story is
that something more or less works and it makes

00:20:02.840 --> 00:20:08.010
a million copies of itself and there's tiny
variations in those copies often at least

00:20:08.010 --> 00:20:12.590
in principle because of copying error which
is where I think the theory is weak.

00:20:12.590 --> 00:20:15.050
And then they all die except for two.

00:20:15.050 --> 00:20:19.890
And so, why didn't those die, and the answer
is well they happened by chance to vary in

00:20:19.890 --> 00:20:24.410
the direction that the environment varied
and so they get to continue and so the reason

00:20:24.410 --> 00:20:28.010
evolution works is that everything dies.

00:20:28.010 --> 00:20:32.560
Everything fails because you think well, keeping
up with a randomly changing environment is

00:20:32.560 --> 00:20:34.860
impossible, so how do you do it?

00:20:34.860 --> 00:20:36.840
Well you do a million things.

00:20:36.840 --> 00:20:42.900
Most of them don't work, but you only have
to succeed enough to propagate yourself once.

00:20:42.900 --> 00:20:49.020
Now, so I think that that’s not exactly
right because I don't believe that our understanding

00:20:49.020 --> 00:20:50.320
of evolution is complete.

00:20:50.320 --> 00:20:52.010
There’s a bunch of things that I don't believe
in.

00:20:52.010 --> 00:20:55.340
One is DNA is pretty damn complex.

00:20:55.340 --> 00:21:00.040
It’s not obvious how it could have evolved,
and even the people who discovered DNA were

00:21:00.040 --> 00:21:03.000
very very complexed that it existed.

00:21:03.000 --> 00:21:06.302
So one of them - and I told you this - thought
that it came from outer space and that it

00:21:06.302 --> 00:21:08.500
had evolved from somewhere else.

00:21:08.500 --> 00:21:15.560
You might think about that as crazy theory
but cosmic winds, like solar winds and that

00:21:15.560 --> 00:21:21.680
kind of thing, can propel tiny things vast
distances relatively rapidly, and over 13.5

00:21:21.680 --> 00:21:24.430
billion years you can really get around.

00:21:24.430 --> 00:21:30.150
I think the estimates, even if you used sub
- if you used transportation methods that

00:21:30.150 --> 00:21:33.950
are about the speed that we have, you can
colonize the whole galaxy in some like 20

00:21:33.950 --> 00:21:39.720
million years because it’s exponential.
20 million years is a tiny tiny subset of

00:21:39.720 --> 00:21:43.740
3.5 billion years so it’s not as far fetched
as it sounds.

00:21:43.740 --> 00:21:45.850
The problem is it doesn't address the problem.

00:21:45.850 --> 00:21:48.720
Okay it came from somewhere else, but how
did it get there?

00:21:48.720 --> 00:21:50.670
It just pushes the problem back.

00:21:50.670 --> 00:21:55.991
But there's this other issue too which is
called epigenesis, and that's really put an

00:21:55.991 --> 00:22:00.640
old spoke in the scientific wheels in the
last twenty years because the theory was,

00:22:00.640 --> 00:22:08.130
twenty years ago, that all evolution preceded
through random mutation, and it was random.

00:22:08.130 --> 00:22:12.510
But it turns out that's not exactly right
because there had been a number of demonstrations

00:22:12.510 --> 00:22:18.400
that if something happens to generation A,
that will produce a transformation in the

00:22:18.400 --> 00:22:25.040
genetic information that's transferred to
generation B. And the transformation often

00:22:25.040 --> 00:22:31.030
seems not merely as a consequence of damage,
but actually a form of adaptation to the new

00:22:31.030 --> 00:22:33.600
environment.

00:22:33.600 --> 00:22:39.610
Now that’s a tough one, like twenty years
ago when I was studying evolutionary psychology

00:22:39.610 --> 00:22:44.810
and evolutionary biology, if someone would
have said, well you can transmit acquired

00:22:44.810 --> 00:22:49.550
characteristics, it would have been like you’re
over there with the creationists because that

00:22:49.550 --> 00:22:50.550
was no go.

00:22:50.550 --> 00:22:52.000
That was against the rules.

00:22:52.000 --> 00:22:57.010
But it does happen, and the fact that epigenesis
happens at all means that there's a lot of

00:22:57.010 --> 00:22:59.680
things about how DNA works that we don't know.

00:22:59.680 --> 00:23:06.660
I actually think what's happening is that
DNA is a very very complex microcomputer,

00:23:06.660 --> 00:23:10.030
and I don’t know exactly how it operates,
maybe it’s a quantum computer.

00:23:10.030 --> 00:23:11.540
It’s very complex.

00:23:11.540 --> 00:23:15.770
Remember it’s had three and a half billion
years to figure out what's going on, and it’s

00:23:15.770 --> 00:23:17.200
pretty good at surviving.

00:23:17.200 --> 00:23:22.290
So you don’t want to underestimate it, and
the structures it builds at the molecular

00:23:22.290 --> 00:23:27.750
level are so complex it just - if you - there’s
some great animations of those things going

00:23:27.750 --> 00:23:29.390
on at the molecular level on the net.

00:23:29.390 --> 00:23:34.010
They're so complex that you can't help but
think of them as computational when you watch

00:23:34.010 --> 00:23:35.010
them.

00:23:35.010 --> 00:23:36.300
They're not random processes man.

00:23:36.300 --> 00:23:42.920
Like I watched the little molecule that checks
for error in a DNA replication string.

00:23:42.920 --> 00:23:48.930
I mean, it checks for errors in a DNA replication
string man.

00:23:48.930 --> 00:23:53.420
To figure out how it does something like that
without some form of computation strikes me

00:23:53.420 --> 00:23:54.780
as impossible.

00:23:54.780 --> 00:23:59.730
Anyways I think what happens is that over
time the DNA plays, like it can manifest itself

00:23:59.730 --> 00:24:03.780
in many different forms and it also capitalizes
on mutation.

00:24:03.780 --> 00:24:05.530
I think that's how it works.

00:24:05.530 --> 00:24:10.960
Now you know there's other I’d say kind
of weak evidence for that.

00:24:10.960 --> 00:24:15.890
Maybe it’s only evidence by analogy but
if I take one of you and I put you in a radically

00:24:15.890 --> 00:24:20.880
new environment, new genes will turn on in
your brain and code for new proteins and so

00:24:20.880 --> 00:24:25.320
it’s like you're sitting there but in some
sense there’s a whole set of potential yous

00:24:25.320 --> 00:24:26.320
that are sitting there.

00:24:26.320 --> 00:24:30.050
A lot of that potential is actually locked
in the genetic code.

00:24:30.050 --> 00:24:34.309
I mean, it’s dependant on the information
that's in the environment around it cause

00:24:34.309 --> 00:24:37.860
it’s not all in the code but it’s enough
to get the process started.

00:24:37.860 --> 00:24:42.790
So if I take you out of situation A and put
you in situation B, you build new brain structures

00:24:42.790 --> 00:24:44.200
at the microlevel.

00:24:44.200 --> 00:24:46.090
So you’re actually a different person.

00:24:46.090 --> 00:24:49.830
That's actually a very interesting way of
thinking about the consequences of something

00:24:49.830 --> 00:24:51.400
like exposure in psychotherapy.

00:24:51.400 --> 00:24:55.920
It’s like okay, you're all timid and there's
eighteen things you're terrified of.

00:24:55.920 --> 00:24:57.510
You go out and master one of them.

00:24:57.510 --> 00:25:00.710
It’s like poof, your brain is the not the
same.

00:25:00.710 --> 00:25:01.800
Maybe you're a little braver.

00:25:01.800 --> 00:25:05.660
And then you go master another one, and a
bunch more of you turns on.

00:25:05.660 --> 00:25:10.600
Now I don't know if one thing turns on, something
else turns off.

00:25:10.600 --> 00:25:14.450
You know what I mean is that there's only
so much of you that can be turned on at any

00:25:14.450 --> 00:25:22.420
one time, but maybe a lot of more you can
be turned on than is turned on, so.

00:25:22.420 --> 00:25:26.650
I think it looks like that because people
who really challenge themselves across the

00:25:26.650 --> 00:25:30.400
span of their life, they develop talents in
multiple directions.

00:25:30.400 --> 00:25:39.010
People can really be amazingly good at a ridiculous
number of things.

00:25:39.010 --> 00:25:48.580
Okay so, Sokolov and Vinogradova showed that
you model the world right down to the finest

00:25:48.580 --> 00:25:55.670
details, but then Dan Simon and his crew showed
“No you don’t, you’re so blind you can't

00:25:55.670 --> 00:25:56.670
even believe it.”

00:25:56.670 --> 00:26:03.020
It’s like okay, those are not the same set
of facts, so how do we reconcile that?

00:26:03.020 --> 00:26:07.020
Well here’s one way of getting a bit of
sense of it.

00:26:07.020 --> 00:26:08.570
Look straight ahead.

00:26:08.570 --> 00:26:15.210
Okay, now concentrate not on what you see
in the middle of your vision but at the sides.

00:26:15.210 --> 00:26:18.000
It’s probably better if you look at a person
to do that, but it doesn’t really matter

00:26:18.000 --> 00:26:19.000
though.

00:26:19.000 --> 00:26:23.940
So look straight ahead but pay attention to
what you see in the periphery.

00:26:23.940 --> 00:26:29.500
So I can tell you what you’ll see because
some of it is very difficult to detect.

00:26:29.500 --> 00:26:33.050
Way out in the periphery, it’s black and
white.

00:26:33.050 --> 00:26:34.630
You can't tell that though.

00:26:34.630 --> 00:26:38.220
But we know that because if we change colors
way out in the periphery on you, you can't

00:26:38.220 --> 00:26:39.750
detect a change.

00:26:39.750 --> 00:26:44.420
And then out to about here, if something isn't
moving, it’s almost impossible to see it.

00:26:44.420 --> 00:26:47.720
But as soon as it moves, you can see that
there’s something moving.

00:26:47.720 --> 00:26:49.740
Sort of like dinosaur vision.

00:26:49.740 --> 00:26:52.140
Remember Jurassic Park?

00:26:52.140 --> 00:26:54.530
Don’t move because the dinosaur can see
you.

00:26:54.530 --> 00:26:59.371
It’s like well, you have dinosaur movement
detector still built into your nervous system.

00:26:59.371 --> 00:27:05.380
And the answer is why, and the answer is well
if you can't afford to have a lot of your

00:27:05.380 --> 00:27:10.841
brain wired up in the periphery of your vision
because your head is only so big, then you

00:27:10.841 --> 00:27:15.200
have to concentrate on only those things that
are most likely to be relevant.

00:27:15.200 --> 00:27:19.270
And moving things are more likely to be relevant
because they can eat you.

00:27:19.270 --> 00:27:23.309
Moving things are more likely to be relevant
than stationary things especially if you've

00:27:23.309 --> 00:27:27.310
been in the environment for a while right,
because if you're in here it’s like the

00:27:27.310 --> 00:27:30.890
stationary things are going to stay stationary
so you can ignore them.

00:27:30.890 --> 00:27:35.950
You don't even have to see them, but the moving
things, they’re transforming at a time rate

00:27:35.950 --> 00:27:39.059
that’s relevant to you so it’s good to
be able to detect those.

00:27:39.059 --> 00:27:45.050
Now the periphery of your vision is actually
wired in more directly to your - to the lower

00:27:45.050 --> 00:27:47.340
levels of your nervous system.

00:27:47.340 --> 00:27:52.081
So it can trigger off things like startle
reflexes which are very rapid, and it can

00:27:52.081 --> 00:27:56.990
also trigger an orienting response which is
the movement of your sensory systems in the

00:27:56.990 --> 00:28:01.910
direction of the stimulus so if something
over here moves, then you go like that, and

00:28:01.910 --> 00:28:04.920
then what you're doing is you're nailing it
with your central vision.

00:28:04.920 --> 00:28:09.840
Now, out in your peripheral vision, if you
follow the optical tract back to your brain,

00:28:09.840 --> 00:28:17.400
you'll see the peripheral retinal nerves don't
attach to a tremendous number of visual cortex

00:28:17.400 --> 00:28:23.980
neurons, but the central ones do so each of
the central neurons in your central vision

00:28:23.980 --> 00:28:24.980
- the fovea.

00:28:24.980 --> 00:28:30.330
Each one of those is connected to ten thousand
neurons at the first level of elaboration,

00:28:30.330 --> 00:28:33.660
and then each neuron is connected to about
ten thousand other neurons.

00:28:33.660 --> 00:28:39.250
So it’s ten thousand to the ten thousandth
two neurons in and so a lot of your brain

00:28:39.250 --> 00:28:44.460
is focusing on that tiny little pinpoint that
you use that’s foveal vision, and it seems

00:28:44.460 --> 00:28:50.780
to be about big enough so that you can bring
a face into high resolution in the center

00:28:50.780 --> 00:28:54.430
and you can track the movement of the various
elements of the faces.

00:28:54.430 --> 00:28:58.900
If you look at someone and you look about
eighteen inches to their left or to their

00:28:58.900 --> 00:29:02.420
right, you'll see that you can't see their
nose anymore.

00:29:02.420 --> 00:29:05.429
You see kind of a vague outline of their face.

00:29:05.429 --> 00:29:06.860
You can still see the color.

00:29:06.860 --> 00:29:10.980
You can detect their eyebrows, and you can
detect their mouth especially if their mouth

00:29:10.980 --> 00:29:12.600
moves or their eyes move.

00:29:12.600 --> 00:29:15.370
You just zero in on that right away.

00:29:15.370 --> 00:29:21.120
What’s happening is that way out here, your
vision is incredibly low resolution and the

00:29:21.120 --> 00:29:27.310
resolution increases to the center where it’s
very very high, and then there's some tweaks

00:29:27.310 --> 00:29:31.650
like okay you can't really see out here, but
you can see motion, and that's good enough

00:29:31.650 --> 00:29:34.870
because as soon as something moves, you can
turn your head cause you can turn your head

00:29:34.870 --> 00:29:39.679
and your eyes and you can focus that little
pinpoint on it and dance around cause that's

00:29:39.679 --> 00:29:41.160
what you do with the pinpoint.

00:29:41.160 --> 00:29:47.570
It moves around very rapidly, and you can
scan that central area with high resolution.

00:29:47.570 --> 00:29:51.860
So it’s an efficient use of limited resources.

00:29:51.860 --> 00:29:57.150
So partly the way that you manage in the world
which is very very complicated, is that even

00:29:57.150 --> 00:29:59.820
your senses simplify it for you.

00:29:59.820 --> 00:30:04.950
There's only - you can't see the entire spectrum
of electromagnetic radiation.

00:30:04.950 --> 00:30:06.750
You can only see the visual light spectrum.

00:30:06.750 --> 00:30:07.750
It’s very narrow.

00:30:07.750 --> 00:30:10.450
There’s a whole range of sounds you can't
hear.

00:30:10.450 --> 00:30:11.940
There's things you can't smell.

00:30:11.940 --> 00:30:15.960
So you're in a box and some things get through,
but not everything.

00:30:15.960 --> 00:30:20.780
So that simplifies things for you right off
the bat and hypothetically those things you

00:30:20.780 --> 00:30:25.059
do detect or those things where it was necessary
for you to detect for you to be here, over

00:30:25.059 --> 00:30:29.210
this vast span of time and no doubt there
were variants all the way through that tried

00:30:29.210 --> 00:30:33.540
to parse it up differently - slightly differently
- and that didn't work out as well so they

00:30:33.540 --> 00:30:34.790
aren't around.

00:30:34.790 --> 00:30:39.720
So you’re around because of death and replication.

00:30:39.720 --> 00:30:45.700
So it’s an interesting take on death, eh,
because you're the product of an unbelievable

00:30:45.700 --> 00:30:47.130
number of deaths.

00:30:47.130 --> 00:30:53.750
Right, because all of those experiments had
to take place in some sense for you to be

00:30:53.750 --> 00:30:55.100
here at all.

00:30:55.100 --> 00:31:03.160
So anyways, okay, then, so you can see with
your central vision that you focus in on one

00:31:03.160 --> 00:31:09.600
point and you reduce the others to low resolution
representations, maybe even cartoons, and

00:31:09.600 --> 00:31:13.160
then I also think, although I don't know this
for sure and someone has undoubtedly done

00:31:13.160 --> 00:31:14.160
the work.

00:31:14.160 --> 00:31:20.280
My suspicions are that in the periphery, you
don't update - you know how your screen updates.

00:31:20.280 --> 00:31:23.460
It’s sixty cycles a second, something like
that.

00:31:23.460 --> 00:31:28.020
You update your vision at about sixty cycles
per second, and we know that because if the

00:31:28.020 --> 00:31:32.981
screen resolution or if the screen update
decreases below sixty cycles a second, people

00:31:32.981 --> 00:31:34.960
can start to see it flicker.

00:31:34.960 --> 00:31:36.220
So we have a refresh rate.

00:31:36.220 --> 00:31:37.840
It’s around sixty hertz.

00:31:37.840 --> 00:31:45.590
Some people can see flicker at higher hertz
than that, but most people can't.

00:31:45.590 --> 00:31:46.890
Sixty is about right.

00:31:46.890 --> 00:31:52.310
So, that's another way that you deal with
the fact that there's way too much for you

00:31:52.310 --> 00:31:54.000
to deal with.

00:31:54.000 --> 00:31:58.960
Your temporal resolution isn't very high,
you know, so the smallest meaningful event

00:31:58.960 --> 00:32:01.250
for you is about a tenth of a second long.

00:32:01.250 --> 00:32:05.470
Now if you're in a car accident or you're
in some situation where it’s an emergency

00:32:05.470 --> 00:32:09.620
and you get a massive burst of dopamine, you
might be able to speed that processing up

00:32:09.620 --> 00:32:10.640
by a hundredfold.

00:32:10.640 --> 00:32:11.680
Who knows?

00:32:11.680 --> 00:32:15.010
But you're gonna be absolutely exhausted instantly
when you do that.

00:32:15.010 --> 00:32:18.370
I don't know if you've ever experienced that
in a car accident or something like that but

00:32:18.370 --> 00:32:26.920
people often report that time slows down dramatically,
but then afterwards they're just done.

00:32:26.920 --> 00:32:31.130
So the reason you don't see a thousand times
as fast as you do is because you’d have

00:32:31.130 --> 00:32:32.570
to be eating like a hummingbird right.

00:32:32.570 --> 00:32:37.100
You'd need a big vat of nectar attached to
you, and you'd just be sipping on it or gulping

00:32:37.100 --> 00:32:38.830
it down nonstop.

00:32:38.830 --> 00:32:43.240
So you're also restricted in terms of your
temporal resolution.

00:32:43.240 --> 00:32:48.660
Okay, well then what else do you do to simplify
the world.

00:32:48.660 --> 00:32:58.429
Well this is where the tool concept of concept,
the tool concept of concept starts to become

00:32:58.429 --> 00:33:04.880
useful because you're actually aiming at things
all the time, and I really like the aim metaphor

00:33:04.880 --> 00:33:10.090
because if you understand the metaphor well,
it really sheds some light on what human beings

00:33:10.090 --> 00:33:12.340
are like.

00:33:12.340 --> 00:33:15.960
Chimps are hunters, but they're not hunters
like human beings are hunters.

00:33:15.960 --> 00:33:17.440
We’re really really good hunters.

00:33:17.440 --> 00:33:21.890
Chimps, they have to kind of mob an animal
and then they’ll tear it apart, and they

00:33:21.890 --> 00:33:24.090
like meat so they’ll definitely do it.

00:33:24.090 --> 00:33:28.929
But human beings have been hunting in an unbelievably
sophisticated way for a tremendous amount

00:33:28.929 --> 00:33:33.740
of time, and there's a bunch of things about
us that aren't like chimps.

00:33:33.740 --> 00:33:37.960
So one is we can throw, and our bodies are
built for throwing.

00:33:37.960 --> 00:33:41.180
Now you might think well that's not that relevant
but if something is chasing you through the

00:33:41.180 --> 00:33:45.299
bush and you can throw a rock at it, it becomes
relevant very quickly.

00:33:45.299 --> 00:33:51.700
And so we’re really good at taking projectiles,
specifying targets, and hurling the projectiles

00:33:51.700 --> 00:33:52.830
at targets.

00:33:52.830 --> 00:33:56.420
And we can do that with rocks, and we can
do it with bows, and we can do it with rifles,

00:33:56.420 --> 00:33:59.960
and we can do it with rifles, and we can do
it with nuclear weapons.

00:33:59.960 --> 00:34:04.190
And so there's something about us that's obsessed
with hitting the middle of the target.

00:34:04.190 --> 00:34:11.290
Now there's an interesting side note for that
which is that the word sin is derived from

00:34:11.290 --> 00:34:17.070
the Greek word “Hamartia,” and hamartia
is an archery term to mean to miss the center

00:34:17.070 --> 00:34:18.790
of the target.

00:34:18.790 --> 00:34:21.300
Now that's something very much worth thinking
about.

00:34:21.300 --> 00:34:23.270
It’s a very lovely concordance.

00:34:23.270 --> 00:34:25.990
So we’re built on this hunting platform.

00:34:25.990 --> 00:34:28.500
We can track moving things extraordinarily
well.

00:34:28.500 --> 00:34:30.020
We have amazing eyesight.

00:34:30.020 --> 00:34:34.570
Our eyesight’s better than any primate by
a huge amount, and better than any other creature

00:34:34.570 --> 00:34:37.080
except for hawks and other birds of prey.

00:34:37.080 --> 00:34:43.380
So, and, we also use - so we have this intense,
amazing capacity to zero in and here’s some

00:34:43.380 --> 00:34:44.680
cool things about your eyes.

00:34:44.680 --> 00:34:48.470
So, if you look at a gorilla, you’ll see
that a gorilla doesn't really have whites

00:34:48.470 --> 00:34:53.040
in their eyes, and you might think well that's
kind of weird because we do, and so what's

00:34:53.040 --> 00:34:54.230
the hypothesis?

00:34:54.230 --> 00:34:59.760
Well, if I’m looking at somebody at the
back of the room, I can tell if they're looking

00:34:59.760 --> 00:35:01.550
at my eyes.

00:35:01.550 --> 00:35:05.430
If they're looking at my nose, it’s almost
as if their face just gets a little bit vaguer

00:35:05.430 --> 00:35:06.430
like we’re not communicating.

00:35:06.430 --> 00:35:08.990
It’s eye to eye contact.

00:35:08.990 --> 00:35:15.359
So our ability to specify the direction in
which another person’s eyes are pointing

00:35:15.359 --> 00:35:17.130
is phenomenal.

00:35:17.130 --> 00:35:18.860
And you might say, well why?

00:35:18.860 --> 00:35:23.000
Well first of all, there’s a lot of expression
around the eyes right.

00:35:23.000 --> 00:35:24.950
So you can imagine coevolution.

00:35:24.950 --> 00:35:28.300
The coevolution is sharper and sharper vision.

00:35:28.300 --> 00:35:33.830
Vision associated with detecting eyes and
then also more and more evolution for emotional

00:35:33.830 --> 00:35:36.080
expression around the eyes.

00:35:36.080 --> 00:35:37.760
So there's that.

00:35:37.760 --> 00:35:41.910
But then, the other thing that I wanted - the
reason that I would be interested in that

00:35:41.910 --> 00:35:46.340
is because I want to know what the hell you’re
up to because maybe you're a friend and maybe

00:35:46.340 --> 00:35:49.991
you're a foe or maybe you're interested in
something that I should be interested in.

00:35:49.991 --> 00:35:54.021
So we know what people are like in that regard,
so you go stand out in the corner and you

00:35:54.021 --> 00:35:58.400
look up, and soon there are ten people you
have around you looking up.

00:35:58.400 --> 00:35:59.400
And why is that?

00:35:59.400 --> 00:36:06.500
It’s because when they see where your eyes
are pointed and embody you, they think well

00:36:06.500 --> 00:36:08.020
that person - they don’t think this.

00:36:08.020 --> 00:36:09.840
It’s the equivalent of thinking.

00:36:09.840 --> 00:36:13.290
They think well that person must be looking
at something interesting because that's what

00:36:13.290 --> 00:36:17.260
people look at, and if it’s interesting
to them then it’s probably going to be interesting

00:36:17.260 --> 00:36:19.120
to me especially if it’s in the sky.

00:36:19.120 --> 00:36:22.010
It’s like what the hell, that’s not supposed
to be happening.

00:36:22.010 --> 00:36:25.600
So you're looking at people’s eyes because
you want to know what they're up to in their

00:36:25.600 --> 00:36:32.050
facial display, which is your face is unbelievably
innervated and controllable, both by conscious

00:36:32.050 --> 00:36:33.710
and by unconscious means.

00:36:33.710 --> 00:36:37.290
That tells you what they're up to, which is
why you want to see someone’s face.

00:36:37.290 --> 00:36:38.780
You don't talk to the back of their head.

00:36:38.780 --> 00:36:42.119
You want to talk to their face, and you don't
like it when they're looking like this or

00:36:42.119 --> 00:36:45.500
looking like that or failing to make eye contact.

00:36:45.500 --> 00:36:48.440
Although too much eye contact is a predatory
stare.

00:36:48.440 --> 00:36:52.120
So you know, you’ve got -
Well you’ve got to get the balance right.

00:36:52.120 --> 00:36:55.690
So now, what about the whites of the eyes?

00:36:55.690 --> 00:36:59.190
Well the theory is that the reason we’ve
developed whites is because it was easier

00:36:59.190 --> 00:37:03.609
to see where someone’s eyes were pointed
if there was a dark circle in a white space,

00:37:03.609 --> 00:37:08.570
and what that basically meant was that all
your ancestors who were a little less fortunate

00:37:08.570 --> 00:37:13.000
on the eye white side either didn't mate or
got killed.

00:37:13.000 --> 00:37:14.690
Probably in misunderstandings.

00:37:14.690 --> 00:37:20.359
So, our eyes stand out against this bright
white background because that helps us - that

00:37:20.359 --> 00:37:24.380
helps other people detect what we’re up
to, and that means we can trust each other

00:37:24.380 --> 00:37:30.180
more easily and we can - even as something
as simple as like eye contact in a situation

00:37:30.180 --> 00:37:35.200
where there’s a possibility of finding a
sexual partner is a very important component

00:37:35.200 --> 00:37:40.350
of that because eye contact indicates interest
and it also indicates, at least in principle,

00:37:40.350 --> 00:37:42.510
the possibility of approach.

00:37:42.510 --> 00:37:48.890
And so approach is dopaminergically mediated
and it’s a positive emotional state.

00:37:48.890 --> 00:37:56.050
And that’s also why, interestingly enough,
if you go into a pharmacy, and there's a rack

00:37:56.050 --> 00:37:59.720
of magazines, and they're - what's on the
cover?

00:37:59.720 --> 00:38:00.730
Always.

00:38:00.730 --> 00:38:01.770
Almost always.

00:38:01.770 --> 00:38:03.290
Beautiful women.

00:38:03.290 --> 00:38:07.660
Like on fifty percent of the men’s magazines
there's a beautiful woman and on like a hundred

00:38:07.660 --> 00:38:11.740
percent of the women’s magazines which is
really interesting you know because you might

00:38:11.740 --> 00:38:13.830
think well why is that?

00:38:13.830 --> 00:38:18.440
Women’s eyes are always looking out in a
way that they're looking at you.

00:38:18.440 --> 00:38:25.060
So there was some interest - so it’s an
invocation of interest, and so, you know,

00:38:25.060 --> 00:38:26.060
magazines evolve.

00:38:26.060 --> 00:38:28.030
All the ones that don’t get bought fail.

00:38:28.030 --> 00:38:31.340
And so what’s happened is they've all converged
to the same point, and the same point is that

00:38:31.340 --> 00:38:38.010
the thing that's maximally interesting to
a magazine purchaser is a beautiful female

00:38:38.010 --> 00:38:39.010
face.

00:38:39.010 --> 00:38:40.010
Whether it’s male or female.

00:38:40.010 --> 00:38:43.930
Now on the male side, there's also gadgets
of all sorts.

00:38:43.930 --> 00:38:47.200
And that may be that men are more gadgets
oriented than women.

00:38:47.200 --> 00:38:54.100
So anyways, and we also know that for example
with men, if you show them - this is a funny

00:38:54.100 --> 00:38:55.910
little study.

00:38:55.910 --> 00:39:01.610
So you show them a face of a beautiful woman
and her eyes are looking that way, or this

00:39:01.610 --> 00:39:06.400
way or they're looking right at him, you can
check the activity in the dopaminergic center

00:39:06.400 --> 00:39:11.109
in a place called the nucleus accumbens which
is the same that cocaine hits.

00:39:11.109 --> 00:39:13.540
Face on eye contact, that thing lights up.

00:39:13.540 --> 00:39:16.619
It lights up even more if she’s wearing
a red dress.

00:39:16.619 --> 00:39:21.940
Right, and you can get the same kind of lighting
up with a red curvy sports car.

00:39:21.940 --> 00:39:22.940
Yeah.

00:39:22.940 --> 00:39:29.580
So it’s the, you know - my thought when
I read that was the perfect situation is like

00:39:29.580 --> 00:39:35.810
a beautiful girl dressed in red perched on
a sports car, with some cocaine.

00:39:35.810 --> 00:39:39.750
Why red?

00:39:39.750 --> 00:39:40.930
Why red?

00:39:40.930 --> 00:39:42.369
That’s a good question.

00:39:42.369 --> 00:39:44.680
I’m not exactly sure why - oh that's wrong.

00:39:44.680 --> 00:39:47.930
I do know why red.

00:39:47.930 --> 00:39:50.829
Ripe fruit.

00:39:50.829 --> 00:39:53.000
Women have coevolved with ripe fruit.

00:39:53.000 --> 00:39:55.820
It’s very sneaky of you by the way.

00:39:55.820 --> 00:39:57.191
So that's why - you know that too.

00:39:57.191 --> 00:39:59.760
You just have to leaf through a women’s
magazine.

00:39:59.760 --> 00:40:04.090
Lipstick is always associated with like apples
that are glistening in some way, or a peach

00:40:04.090 --> 00:40:05.700
or something like that.

00:40:05.700 --> 00:40:11.760
And we were primarily fruit eaters, and the
reason we have color vision is to detect fruit,

00:40:11.760 --> 00:40:13.190
ripe fruit.

00:40:13.190 --> 00:40:17.619
So part of the reason that - also part of
the reason that fruit turns red or colors

00:40:17.619 --> 00:40:22.150
when it ripens is because the fruit that was
successfully eaten by creatures that distributed

00:40:22.150 --> 00:40:26.530
the seeds was the fruit that was ripe when
it was eaten.

00:40:26.530 --> 00:40:31.150
So as the color vision evolved, maybe there
was a red tint for only god knows what reason,

00:40:31.150 --> 00:40:35.760
then a positive feedback loop developed and
fruit got redder and redder, and at the same

00:40:35.760 --> 00:40:38.220
time, women capitalized on that.

00:40:38.220 --> 00:40:43.340
So that’s partly, I think, what explains
the association between Eve and the apple

00:40:43.340 --> 00:40:48.650
in Genesis because Genesis is also a story
about gaining sight.

00:40:48.650 --> 00:40:50.260
So yeah, that’s why red.

00:40:50.260 --> 00:40:51.790
That’s pretty funny.

00:40:51.790 --> 00:40:53.940
I really think that's pretty funny.

00:40:53.940 --> 00:41:00.260
You know, evolved sensory significance is
absolutely everywhere.

00:41:00.260 --> 00:41:05.369
It’s so deep inside of us.

00:41:05.369 --> 00:41:07.030
Yeah.

00:41:07.030 --> 00:41:09.030
I have to wonder about blood too, that seems
like it also

00:41:09.030 --> 00:41:10.030
Oh yeah, yeah.

00:41:10.030 --> 00:41:13.700
Red’s a significant color, but I think - I
mean and it’s funny too because say there’s

00:41:13.700 --> 00:41:17.099
a flushed face, well that means it’s infused
with blood.

00:41:17.099 --> 00:41:18.780
It’s also a sign of health.

00:41:18.780 --> 00:41:21.060
It’s also a sign of sexual arousal.

00:41:21.060 --> 00:41:27.060
But it is a sign of health, and so you see
in old Coke ads from the 1930’s, the girl’s

00:41:27.060 --> 00:41:30.349
cheeks are so red it looks like she has a
fever.

00:41:30.349 --> 00:41:36.340
So it’s a form of sexual signalling that
indicates health.

00:41:36.340 --> 00:41:37.340
So that's another part.

00:41:37.340 --> 00:41:41.150
I mean these are multiply determined right,
as anything as complex as vision.

00:41:41.150 --> 00:41:45.320
Yeah, and blood means “Hey, maybe don’t
go there” or maybe it means there’s something

00:41:45.320 --> 00:41:46.320
to eat.

00:41:46.320 --> 00:41:47.320
Right, right.

00:41:47.320 --> 00:41:48.320
Yup.

00:41:48.320 --> 00:41:52.859
There was another theory with color sight,
and it had to do with people’s facial expressions

00:41:52.859 --> 00:42:00.030
- not expressions but depending on whether
you're angry or aroused.

00:42:00.030 --> 00:42:04.380
Oh definitely, you can see that those things
would loop together.

00:42:04.380 --> 00:42:10.410
Once the color vision starts to adapt, the
color vision for fruit is way older, I would

00:42:10.410 --> 00:42:14.580
suspect, because when we developed it, we
had fur.

00:42:14.580 --> 00:42:19.540
But once the ability’s there and we lose
our facial fur, it’s possible to capitalize

00:42:19.540 --> 00:42:21.990
on the ability to detect the signalling.

00:42:21.990 --> 00:42:26.370
And you know, we’re talking about a transformation
that's over about - we were living in trees

00:42:26.370 --> 00:42:28.590
about sixty million years ago.

00:42:28.590 --> 00:42:32.500
So the transition between us and them is sixty
million years.

00:42:32.500 --> 00:42:36.180
The separation between chimps is about seven.

00:42:36.180 --> 00:42:45.350
And you know, chimp skin is so dark, you’re
not gonna get a lot of signalling.

00:42:45.350 --> 00:42:48.250
Okay so, back to this little diagram.

00:42:48.250 --> 00:42:51.320
Now here’s the idea.

00:42:51.320 --> 00:42:57.030
This is deeply informed, although I didn't
know it when I was working on these ideas,

00:42:57.030 --> 00:43:03.500
this is deeply informed by a guy named Norbert
Wiener who was one of the twentieth century’s

00:43:03.500 --> 00:43:09.990
massive geniuses and a force behind the development
of computation.

00:43:09.990 --> 00:43:11.750
So this is a cybernetic model.

00:43:11.750 --> 00:43:13.610
He invented cybernetics.

00:43:13.610 --> 00:43:19.740
Now I didn't learn this from Norbert Wiener
because I learned it from Jeffrey Gray, but

00:43:19.740 --> 00:43:23.520
Jeffrey Gray’s source had learned it from
Norbert Wiener way back in the 1940’s.

00:43:23.520 --> 00:43:28.370
In fact there was a convention back in the
1940’s that Wiener presented many of his

00:43:28.370 --> 00:43:32.950
ideas at that many psychologists attended,
including Jean Piaget.

00:43:32.950 --> 00:43:37.349
So Piaget’s theory is very much influenced
by cybernetics as well.

00:43:37.349 --> 00:43:43.050
Many many of the standard psychology cognitive
models are cybernetic.

00:43:43.050 --> 00:43:46.990
So they're grounded in thinking that's grounded
in the engineering thinking and not psychological

00:43:46.990 --> 00:43:48.170
thinking at all.

00:43:48.170 --> 00:43:49.170
Yup.

00:43:49.170 --> 00:43:53.100
Isn’t Humberd’s book called Cybernetics?]

00:43:53.100 --> 00:43:54.750
No, Dianetics.

00:43:54.750 --> 00:43:56.400
Yeah, yeah.

00:43:56.400 --> 00:44:04.030
Okay so, how might you conceptualize the fundamental
elements of a personality.

00:44:04.030 --> 00:44:06.890
Well, this is what it looks like to me.

00:44:06.890 --> 00:44:10.190
The first thing is you're a moving creature.

00:44:10.190 --> 00:44:11.280
So here's a funny story.

00:44:11.280 --> 00:44:17.540
There's a little animal whose name I forget,
and in its - it’s an ocean animal - in its

00:44:17.540 --> 00:44:20.380
juvenile state, it swims around.

00:44:20.380 --> 00:44:26.060
Okay so in its juvenile state, it has to coordinate
its movements and interact with the changing

00:44:26.060 --> 00:44:27.450
environment.

00:44:27.450 --> 00:44:29.020
So it has a brain.

00:44:29.020 --> 00:44:33.500
But when it turns into an adult, it attaches
itself to a rock and turns into something

00:44:33.500 --> 00:44:35.120
like a piece of coral.

00:44:35.120 --> 00:44:38.230
It promptly digests its own brain.

00:44:38.230 --> 00:44:42.440
So the issue is unless you don't really need
a brain if you're not gonna go anywhere right.

00:44:42.440 --> 00:44:47.390
So a brain is a going places thing, and we
have a very big brain and so it’s like we’re

00:44:47.390 --> 00:44:51.960
always zooming around doing things, and we've
got these hands that enable us to manipulate

00:44:51.960 --> 00:44:53.530
things and transform things.

00:44:53.530 --> 00:44:58.030
So the brain is for doing things, it’s not
just for perceiving.

00:44:58.030 --> 00:45:03.030
Roughly speaking, the back half of your brain
is for perception, the cortical part.

00:45:03.030 --> 00:45:05.700
Mostly visual perception, but some auditory
and touch.

00:45:05.700 --> 00:45:09.880
A lot of visual, and the front part is for
output.

00:45:09.880 --> 00:45:11.109
Motor action.

00:45:11.109 --> 00:45:16.240
And as you proceed - imagine the front part
starts halfway here.

00:45:16.240 --> 00:45:21.420
As you proceed from the midline forward, the
action becomes more and more abstract until

00:45:21.420 --> 00:45:26.170
when you're dealing with the dorsolateral
prefrontal cortex, you're thinking abstractly.

00:45:26.170 --> 00:45:30.180
Now people think that when they're thinking
abstractly, they're thinking about representations

00:45:30.180 --> 00:45:35.320
of the world because we think about objects,
but that isn't what we’re doing.

00:45:35.320 --> 00:45:40.230
What we’re doing is rehearsing potential
schemas of action.

00:45:40.230 --> 00:45:44.790
So we have this abstract space, which you
might think about as a duplicate world.

00:45:44.790 --> 00:45:46.220
You have an idea.

00:45:46.220 --> 00:45:51.580
You test the idea like you would test it if
you were acting in the world, and the consequence

00:45:51.580 --> 00:45:56.660
of the imaginary testing is if you encounter
grief, then you don't implement the idea in

00:45:56.660 --> 00:45:57.730
the world.

00:45:57.730 --> 00:46:01.270
Now you know, that doesn't always work because
there's trade offs between short term pleasure

00:46:01.270 --> 00:46:06.250
and long term punishment that makes it complicated,
but what seems to have happened is that as

00:46:06.250 --> 00:46:10.810
we've evolved, we’re very sneaky creatures
you know.

00:46:10.810 --> 00:46:15.050
For billions of years, the only way that you
could figure out whether you're right or wrong

00:46:15.050 --> 00:46:18.760
was to go out and do something, and if you
died you were wrong.

00:46:18.760 --> 00:46:24.630
But now, you can think about doing something
and if the consequence of running the simulation

00:46:24.630 --> 00:46:28.570
in your head is that you die, then you don't
do it.

00:46:28.570 --> 00:46:34.910
So what you're basically doing is spinning
off multiple potential use in multiple potential

00:46:34.910 --> 00:46:37.480
universes and running them as simulations.

00:46:37.480 --> 00:46:41.369
Now we really do that now because we’ve
externalized it onto the computer which is

00:46:41.369 --> 00:46:44.090
what we’re doing with really really complex
video games.

00:46:44.090 --> 00:46:45.960
So it’s so smart.

00:46:45.960 --> 00:46:48.710
It’s an amazing evolutionary advance.

00:46:48.710 --> 00:46:54.070
Now it doesn't work that well because sometimes
you run the simulation and you think, this

00:46:54.070 --> 00:46:58.590
is a stupid idea, and you promptly have eight
beers so that you can go do it.

00:46:58.590 --> 00:47:04.390
But, all things considered, that’s still
what this abstract space is for.

00:47:04.390 --> 00:47:08.850
I should tell you something about this too
because you're gonna hear from neuropsychologists

00:47:08.850 --> 00:47:14.119
that a lot of what the dorsolateral prefrontal
cortex does is - the cortex in general - is

00:47:14.119 --> 00:47:16.930
impulse control, and that’s wrong.

00:47:16.930 --> 00:47:19.210
That is not what it does.

00:47:19.210 --> 00:47:24.280
And part of the reason I know this is because
the correlation between prefrontal cortical

00:47:24.280 --> 00:47:29.180
cognitive ability which is very tightly associated
with IQ by the way.

00:47:29.180 --> 00:47:33.490
The correlation is like 0.9 so you can even
argue it’s the same thing.

00:47:33.490 --> 00:47:34.950
Fluid IQ in particular.

00:47:34.950 --> 00:47:43.330
The correlation between dorsolateral prefrontal
cognitive capacity and conscientiousness which

00:47:43.330 --> 00:47:51.510
is like long term goal directed, future oriented,
planned activity is zero.

00:47:51.510 --> 00:47:54.619
Okay zero is a very bad correlation.

00:47:54.619 --> 00:47:59.800
You know the old idea, correlation doesn’t
imply causality.

00:47:59.800 --> 00:48:00.980
Well, wrong.

00:48:00.980 --> 00:48:03.099
It does strongly imply causality.

00:48:03.099 --> 00:48:08.180
It doesn't demonstrate it because with every
causal relationship, there's a correlation

00:48:08.180 --> 00:48:13.340
so a hundred percent of causally related items
have a correlation right.

00:48:13.340 --> 00:48:15.530
A hundred percent correlation.

00:48:15.530 --> 00:48:20.330
Well not necessarily, but it implies correlation
but it does not prove it.

00:48:20.330 --> 00:48:22.750
But zero.

00:48:22.750 --> 00:48:25.170
That’s bad.

00:48:25.170 --> 00:48:26.760
That means those things aren't related.

00:48:26.760 --> 00:48:29.790
Now, if you think about it, you can understand
why.

00:48:29.790 --> 00:48:37.280
They have to be unrelated because if you embodied
your abstractions, then you wouldn't be able

00:48:37.280 --> 00:48:38.700
to experiment with them.

00:48:38.700 --> 00:48:41.560
You’d just act them out and then bang you’d
be dead.

00:48:41.560 --> 00:48:46.820
So you have to separate the cognitive activity
from the embodiment completely so you can

00:48:46.820 --> 00:48:51.560
choose which of your potential selves to incarnate
so to speak.

00:48:51.560 --> 00:48:57.750
Now, the prefrontal cortex is a complex place,
and the lower parts of it seem to be more

00:48:57.750 --> 00:49:03.500
related to the relation between cognitive
activity and the control of behavior.

00:49:03.500 --> 00:49:08.180
But we have tried fifty tasks, I bet, trying
to get a correlation with conscientiousness

00:49:08.180 --> 00:49:10.700
and we haven't found one.

00:49:10.700 --> 00:49:11.710
We cannot.

00:49:11.710 --> 00:49:14.810
The reason I’m telling you this is because
conscientiousness is a very good predictor

00:49:14.810 --> 00:49:18.170
of long term life success.

00:49:18.170 --> 00:49:20.089
You guys get your grades for two reasons.

00:49:20.089 --> 00:49:25.160
One, you're smart - assuming you are - and
two, that you work hard - assuming that you

00:49:25.160 --> 00:49:26.160
do.

00:49:26.160 --> 00:49:28.510
That eats up almost all the variance in your
grades.

00:49:28.510 --> 00:49:33.609
Creativity is zero correlation with grades
at U of T, by the way which I think is extraordinarily

00:49:33.609 --> 00:49:37.020
comical, and completely unsurprising.

00:49:37.020 --> 00:49:43.890
It’s hard for an institution to grade creativity
cause it’s an institution, and the problem

00:49:43.890 --> 00:49:48.859
with being creative is that you force people
to come up with a new grading scheme.

00:49:48.859 --> 00:49:51.150
It’s like no, they're not gonna do that.

00:49:51.150 --> 00:49:54.270
Right, because you come up with something
creative, it’s different.

00:49:54.270 --> 00:49:55.270
Well what do you do with that?

00:49:55.270 --> 00:49:56.270
You say, don’t do that.

00:49:56.270 --> 00:49:58.520
That’s not what I want, even if it’s a
great thing.

00:49:58.520 --> 00:50:01.580
It’s like yeah yeah, take your great thing
and go somewhere else with it.

00:50:01.580 --> 00:50:04.740
I’ve got five minutes to mark your damn
paper.

00:50:04.740 --> 00:50:14.100
So anyways, this capacity for abstraction
is divorced almost completely from embodiment,

00:50:14.100 --> 00:50:16.020
and that's a good thing because that allows
us to experiment.

00:50:16.020 --> 00:50:21.480
But it’s a bad thing because actually doing
the embodiment turns out to be very difficult.

00:50:21.480 --> 00:50:25.349
It’s like your body’s got all these habits
built into it that you've been working on

00:50:25.349 --> 00:50:26.740
for a long time.

00:50:26.740 --> 00:50:28.030
Here's another piece of advice.

00:50:28.030 --> 00:50:34.060
Do not practice things that you don't want
to become because one of the things that happens.

00:50:34.060 --> 00:50:40.900
Let’s say you take Danish speakers and you
play Danish backwards to them, and you do

00:50:40.900 --> 00:50:42.160
a scan of brain activity.

00:50:42.160 --> 00:50:45.550
It’s like their brain’s lighting up like
mad because they don’t know how to say Danish

00:50:45.550 --> 00:50:50.570
backwards, so you get a whole bunch of brain
involved in a new task.

00:50:50.570 --> 00:50:56.869
But then as you practice the task, and routinize
it, less and less brain is involved.

00:50:56.869 --> 00:51:01.480
So what happens is - this is in your standard,
right-hand male and the reason I’m doing

00:51:01.480 --> 00:51:06.369
that as an example is that’s kind of the
simplest default brain in a sense.

00:51:06.369 --> 00:51:10.690
Women are more diffusely organized, and so
it’s harder to exactly talk about their

00:51:10.690 --> 00:51:17.170
neurological structure in a simple way, but
in a right-handed male, what happens is the

00:51:17.170 --> 00:51:22.070
activity starts to shift to the left, and
then it shifts backwards, and as it does so,

00:51:22.070 --> 00:51:25.960
the amount of tissue that gets activated gets
smaller and smaller and smaller and smaller

00:51:25.960 --> 00:51:30.360
until you've built a little machine that takes
care of that sort of thing.

00:51:30.360 --> 00:51:32.770
But the thing about that machine is that it’s
a machine.

00:51:32.770 --> 00:51:35.110
It’s a habit, and it’s actually there.

00:51:35.110 --> 00:51:37.340
It’s built into you.

00:51:37.340 --> 00:51:41.590
So be careful what you practise because if
you practise it enough, it becomes - it’s

00:51:41.590 --> 00:51:42.590
like it’s you.

00:51:42.590 --> 00:51:43.970
It’s the default you.

00:51:43.970 --> 00:51:49.640
And then if you want to change it, you can't
just undo that stupid machine.

00:51:49.640 --> 00:51:50.640
It’s there permanently.

00:51:50.640 --> 00:51:55.670
What you have to do is you have to build another
little machine that shuts it off, and then

00:51:55.670 --> 00:51:59.010
another machine that does the other thing,
and then if you get stressed the little machine

00:51:59.010 --> 00:52:04.380
that shuts the first machine off will stop
working and back your stupid habit will come.

00:52:04.380 --> 00:52:09.530
So, people used to talk about the development
of character, and there was something about

00:52:09.530 --> 00:52:15.580
that that was absolutely right because character
is how you build yourself across time, and

00:52:15.580 --> 00:52:17.450
it really really matters.

00:52:17.450 --> 00:52:19.790
Only practise what you want to become.

00:52:19.790 --> 00:52:22.920
If you practise other things, then well you’ll
become them.

00:52:22.920 --> 00:52:25.230
That’s not so good.

00:52:25.230 --> 00:52:28.820
Okay, so back to this.

00:52:28.820 --> 00:52:31.599
Now, Gray and Sokolov and Vinogradova.

00:52:31.599 --> 00:52:33.359
We’re gonna talk about them for a minute.

00:52:33.359 --> 00:52:36.210
Now, they used to talk about animals.

00:52:36.210 --> 00:52:41.950
They're mostly thinking about animals - rats,
mostly - as having an expectancy model.

00:52:41.950 --> 00:52:43.560
So you take a rat.

00:52:43.560 --> 00:52:46.630
You throw him in a new environment.

00:52:46.630 --> 00:52:47.740
What does he do?

00:52:47.740 --> 00:52:52.260
Well the first he does is freeze because god
only knows what’s out there.

00:52:52.260 --> 00:52:55.510
So it’s like paralyzed rat; he’s just
seen the Medusa.

00:52:55.510 --> 00:53:01.580
He’s paralyzed, and then if nothing attacks
him, he starts to calm down a bit and then

00:53:01.580 --> 00:53:04.550
he’ll start sniffing because rats are all
smell.

00:53:04.550 --> 00:53:08.630
And he sniffs around, and he’s trying to
detect cats, say.

00:53:08.630 --> 00:53:11.950
Now, rats have such a good sense of smell.

00:53:11.950 --> 00:53:15.950
If a rat eats somethign that makes it sick,
it’ll never eat it again.

00:53:15.950 --> 00:53:20.820
But, if a rat smells the breath of a rat that
ate something that made it sick, he won’t

00:53:20.820 --> 00:53:21.820
eat that either.

00:53:21.820 --> 00:53:23.869
Right, so rats eh.

00:53:23.869 --> 00:53:25.869
Man, they can smell things.

00:53:25.869 --> 00:53:29.890
And animals are built around a smell brain,
so they're a lot different than us.

00:53:29.890 --> 00:53:35.070
We have - did I tell you about the t-shirt
study of symmetrical men?

00:53:35.070 --> 00:53:38.619
Oh that’s a funny one.

00:53:38.619 --> 00:53:43.839
So we know that one of the things that signify
