01:00:48.920 --> 01:00:51.369
this way, the baby has more fun.

01:00:51.369 --> 01:00:53.660
And so how do you respond to that?

01:00:53.660 --> 01:00:56.610
Well maybe you’re a little peeved because
you're doing perfectly fine with this baby

01:00:56.610 --> 01:01:02.880
and someone’s coming and altering your tickling
micro personality, and you know, if you're

01:01:02.880 --> 01:01:06.150
irritable you’ll say well why don't you
just go off and find a baby of your own to

01:01:06.150 --> 01:01:08.450
play with.

01:01:08.450 --> 01:01:13.130
And if you're not that irritable, you might
say well okay show me what to do.

01:01:13.130 --> 01:01:16.910
There’ll be a tinge of negative emotion
there, generally speaking, depending on how

01:01:16.910 --> 01:01:23.180
the person has approached you but your self
concept can probably handle a micro change

01:01:23.180 --> 01:01:25.290
to your tickling routine.

01:01:25.290 --> 01:01:30.599
Okay, so that’s gonna produce only a little
bit of anxiety and irritation and the reason

01:01:30.599 --> 01:01:40.329
for that is that the threat to you is proportionate
to the magnitude of the system that's being

01:01:40.329 --> 01:01:43.410
modified by the challenge.

01:01:43.410 --> 01:01:48.060
Now this is really useful to know, okay, practically,
and this is why.

01:01:48.060 --> 01:01:54.660
Let’s say you’re having an argument with
your partner and you say, you’re a stupid

01:01:54.660 --> 01:02:00.160
person and you’ve always been a stupid person
and as far as I can tell, as far into the

01:02:00.160 --> 01:02:04.260
future as I can see, you’re going to remain
a stupid person.

01:02:04.260 --> 01:02:05.339
So what are they supposed to do?

01:02:05.339 --> 01:02:07.940
What are they gonna do when you say that?

01:02:07.940 --> 01:02:11.730
They’re gonna cry like if you mean it.

01:02:11.730 --> 01:02:16.971
They're gonna get angry if you mean it, and
they’ll not like you very much, and why

01:02:16.971 --> 01:02:17.971
is that?

01:02:17.971 --> 01:02:21.180
Well it’s like, it’s assault basically.

01:02:21.180 --> 01:02:28.290
The only thing you can do in a situation like
that is walk away, ignore it, respond in kind,

01:02:28.290 --> 01:02:30.490
or it degenerates into violence.

01:02:30.490 --> 01:02:33.300
That’s it because there's no discussion.

01:02:33.300 --> 01:02:34.960
You haven't left the person anywhere to go.

01:02:34.960 --> 01:02:39.990
You’ve gone right to the top of their hierarchy
and said everything about you is wrong, and

01:02:39.990 --> 01:02:44.990
worse than that, all the mechanisms that we
could use to correct it won’t work.

01:02:44.990 --> 01:02:46.900
So, those are fighting words.

01:02:46.900 --> 01:02:49.030
So don’t do that unless you want to have
a fight.

01:02:49.030 --> 01:02:51.320
So then you might say, well what would you
do instead?

01:02:51.320 --> 01:02:56.660
And the answer is deliver the least amount
of information you possibly can, and so let’s

01:02:56.660 --> 01:03:01.839
say you come home and your person is watching
TV, and you were kind of hoping they’d greet

01:03:01.839 --> 01:03:03.650
you at the door.

01:03:03.650 --> 01:03:06.890
You shouldn't break down into tears and say
you’re a stupid person, you’ve always

01:03:06.890 --> 01:03:09.800
been a stupid person, and you’re gonna be
a stupid person in the future.

01:03:09.800 --> 01:03:17.020
You should say, I have this peculiarity and
that is when I come home, I don't have enough

01:03:17.020 --> 01:03:19.859
confidence to just be happy.

01:03:19.859 --> 01:03:24.369
I would like you to come and say - just shut
the TV off for two minutes, come to the front

01:03:24.369 --> 01:03:28.280
door and say “Hello”, and then you can
go back and watch your TV.

01:03:28.280 --> 01:03:30.069
Would it be okay if you did that?

01:03:30.069 --> 01:03:36.000
And they'll think, “Okay you’ll have to
pay for it somehow,” but then they'll probably

01:03:36.000 --> 01:03:37.510
do it.

01:03:37.510 --> 01:03:42.130
But the thing is you’ve got to specify the
routine that you want transformed at the highest

01:03:42.130 --> 01:03:48.170
possible level of resolution, and you want
to recommend the minimal necessary change

01:03:48.170 --> 01:03:50.359
that will satisfy you.

01:03:50.359 --> 01:03:56.050
So you can't say, “If you love me, you'd
know how to greet me at the door.”

01:03:56.050 --> 01:03:57.050
Ha.

01:03:57.050 --> 01:03:58.460
Not helpful because they're stupid.

01:03:58.460 --> 01:04:01.560
Right, and so are you.

01:04:01.560 --> 01:04:03.089
So you have to spell it out.

01:04:03.089 --> 01:04:04.200
It’s like what do you want.

01:04:04.200 --> 01:04:09.880
Exactly, what would make you satisfied, and
then you have to have your person grudgingly

01:04:09.880 --> 01:04:15.300
practice that a few times and you have to
let them do it very badly and also in a bad

01:04:15.300 --> 01:04:19.910
temper, and then you'd have to reward them
for it and then maybe three months later,

01:04:19.910 --> 01:04:21.829
they'll do it properly.

01:04:21.829 --> 01:04:24.560
So you need to know that because that's what
people are like.

01:04:24.560 --> 01:04:27.940
It’s very hard for them to learn new things,
and they're very resistant to it, but they're

01:04:27.940 --> 01:04:30.640
very responsive to reward.

01:04:30.640 --> 01:04:33.339
So another thing - this is partly what B.F.

01:04:33.339 --> 01:04:37.890
Skinner figured out so, when he was training
rats and he wanted a rat to do something,

01:04:37.890 --> 01:04:43.560
one of the things he would do is put - maybe
he was gonna train the rat to climb up a little

01:04:43.560 --> 01:04:47.819
ladder, and I mean he can get rats to climb
ladders and climb across the little monkey

01:04:47.819 --> 01:04:51.569
bars and then spin around three times and
then, you know, whack a ball and then eat

01:04:51.569 --> 01:04:55.430
something, like he got incredibly complex
behavior out of rats.

01:04:55.430 --> 01:04:58.069
And the way he did that was patience.

01:04:58.069 --> 01:05:02.440
So he put the ladder in the cage, and the
rat would just run around doing rat things,

01:05:02.440 --> 01:05:10.680
and it would put its hand on the first rung,
and Skinner would give it a pellet, and so

01:05:10.680 --> 01:05:16.359
after even once, the rat’s gonna be like
standing in the immediate vicinity of the

01:05:16.359 --> 01:05:20.360
ladder and then the frequency with which its
gonna go like this has just increased so its

01:05:20.360 --> 01:05:21.360
gonna do it again.

01:05:21.360 --> 01:05:22.360
Bang, pellet.

01:05:22.360 --> 01:05:25.359
Well soon the rat’s just going like this,
right?

01:05:25.359 --> 01:05:28.510
So then, you wait until the rat tries the
other hand.

01:05:28.510 --> 01:05:29.869
So you give it a pellet then.

01:05:29.869 --> 01:05:33.200
Well then it’s going like this and then
because it’s going to get bored, it’ll

01:05:33.200 --> 01:05:34.200
go like this.

01:05:34.200 --> 01:05:35.200
It’ll hit the next stair.

01:05:35.200 --> 01:05:36.200
Bang, you give it a pellet.

01:05:36.200 --> 01:05:39.580
Soon the rat is climbing and doing all the
little things he wanted to do.

01:05:39.580 --> 01:05:42.260
Now the problem with that is you have to be
patient.

01:05:42.260 --> 01:05:44.730
You have to wait until the rat does what you
want.

01:05:44.730 --> 01:05:46.910
Okay that’s more relationship advice.

01:05:46.910 --> 01:05:53.390
Wait till the rat does what you want, and
then reward it, and it’s unbelievably useful.

01:05:53.390 --> 01:05:55.810
It’s also extraordinarily positive.

01:05:55.810 --> 01:06:02.849
I mean I’m being, you know, comical, insofar
as I can manage that, about the situation,

01:06:02.849 --> 01:06:07.000
but people love reward and they love attention.

01:06:07.000 --> 01:06:12.420
People love attention more than anything else,
and so if you watch through the day, and when

01:06:12.420 --> 01:06:17.420
your partner does something that’s good,
say “Bam, that was good” or something

01:06:17.420 --> 01:06:18.420
like that.

01:06:18.420 --> 01:06:22.700
You can be inventive, and then they'll do
it more, and if you do that a whole bunch

01:06:22.700 --> 01:06:27.460
like for a year, they'll be doing things that
are good for you just all the time.

01:06:27.460 --> 01:06:33.260
But you have to be patient, which is very
annoying and you have to suppress your response

01:06:33.260 --> 01:06:38.450
to only respond to negative things, you know,
because what we know about the expectancy

01:06:38.450 --> 01:06:46.210
models is that a deviation from expectation
produces a burst of negative emotion.

01:06:46.210 --> 01:06:52.640
So you come home, and the whole house is clean,
but there’s like - I don’t know - the

01:06:52.640 --> 01:06:55.600
dog has shed on the rug or something and the
person overlooked that.

01:06:55.600 --> 01:06:58.990
It’s like you're not gonna see the clean
house, you’re gonna see the rug with the

01:06:58.990 --> 01:07:02.670
dog fur on it and you’re gonna say “Why
didn't you clean up the rug with the dog fur?”

01:07:02.670 --> 01:07:06.500
And they're gonna say, “Good luck getting
me to clean the house again.”

01:07:06.500 --> 01:07:12.670
And you know, because the thing is the exception
stands out and what’s done doesn’t, and

01:07:12.670 --> 01:07:15.690
the reason for that is you can just ignore
what’s done because it’s done.

01:07:15.690 --> 01:07:16.890
It doesn't get in your way.

01:07:16.890 --> 01:07:19.170
So it just gets invisible really quickly.

01:07:19.170 --> 01:07:22.510
So you really gotta watch that tendency.

01:07:22.510 --> 01:07:26.340
One of the things Nietzsche said was that
if you really want to punish someone, you

01:07:26.340 --> 01:07:30.281
don't punish them when they do something wrong
because they expect that.

01:07:30.281 --> 01:07:32.260
That’s not a punishment, they expect that.

01:07:32.260 --> 01:07:34.810
They might even be relieved by it.

01:07:34.810 --> 01:07:39.490
You want to punish them when they do something
right because then you'll really hurt them.

01:07:39.490 --> 01:07:42.690
So that's something to think about, if you're
in a relationship man.

01:07:42.690 --> 01:07:46.820
If someone’s done something right, do not
punish them.

01:07:46.820 --> 01:07:48.859
You do that two or three times and that’s
it man.

01:07:48.859 --> 01:07:51.990
You’re not gonna get them to do that anymore.

01:07:51.990 --> 01:07:54.510
So, judiciousness.

01:07:54.510 --> 01:07:55.510
Watch what they're doing.

01:07:55.510 --> 01:07:59.760
If something happens that’s good, notice
it, and you know, if they’ve done a bunch

01:07:59.760 --> 01:08:02.690
of things don’t concentrate on the things
they did wrong.

01:08:02.690 --> 01:08:04.450
That’s not smart.

01:08:04.450 --> 01:08:06.970
It’s really hard on them too.

01:08:06.970 --> 01:08:11.430
In some sense this sounds manipulative and
selfish, you know, because I’m teaching

01:08:11.430 --> 01:08:18.069
you how to train your partner, but you should
also teach them how to train you because it

01:08:18.069 --> 01:08:21.020
would be really nice if you could come home
and the person would say, “Well what did

01:08:21.020 --> 01:08:22.020
you do today?”

01:08:22.020 --> 01:08:23.689
and you say “Here’s a bunch of things
I did.”

01:08:23.689 --> 01:08:27.209
and they say “Well this looks really good,
and that was great, and why don't you do some

01:08:27.209 --> 01:08:28.209
more of that?”

01:08:28.209 --> 01:08:31.909
And you’re like “Oh boy,” it was a great
day.

01:08:31.909 --> 01:08:37.319
So, you can train them to train you properly,
and that's a really helpful thing especially

01:08:37.319 --> 01:08:40.119
when you do it over a few years.

01:08:40.119 --> 01:08:43.319
That's how you have a good relationship because
you're both clueless as hell to begin with.

01:08:43.319 --> 01:08:48.189
You don’t know even what would make you
happy, much less what would make the other

01:08:48.189 --> 01:08:53.429
person happy and so you've got to figure these
things out bit by bit and you have to inform

01:08:53.429 --> 01:08:59.189
each other, and you have to be patient enough
to let your partner do these things really

01:08:59.189 --> 01:09:00.189
badly.

01:09:00.189 --> 01:09:02.829
I’ll give you another example.

01:09:02.829 --> 01:09:08.179
Sometimes I see couples sporadically in my
clinical practice.

01:09:08.179 --> 01:09:15.299
I’m not a couples counsellor, but sometimes
when I'm working with someone, there's an

01:09:15.299 --> 01:09:19.639
issue that needs to be discussed with both
people because otherwise it’s just stupid,

01:09:19.639 --> 01:09:25.599
and one of the things I often recommend to
people especially once they have kids is that

01:09:25.599 --> 01:09:31.439
they set aside - to use an anachronistic phrase
- date nights.

01:09:31.439 --> 01:09:34.009
Well, everyone hates that idea.

01:09:34.009 --> 01:09:38.239
They say, well you know, they’ll just say,
“I’m not doing that.”

01:09:38.239 --> 01:09:39.239
That’d be one objection.

01:09:39.239 --> 01:09:40.839
“We’re just not doing that.

01:09:40.839 --> 01:09:45.579
That’s what we did before we got married.”

01:09:45.579 --> 01:09:48.089
They'll say, “Well my partner will never
go for that.”

01:09:48.089 --> 01:09:53.909
They've got a bunch of excuses why that isn't
going to work, and so I've heard all those

01:09:53.909 --> 01:09:54.909
excuses.

01:09:54.909 --> 01:09:58.550
I’ve heard all of them, and then maybe I
convince them, “Yeah, yeah, I know this

01:09:58.550 --> 01:09:59.550
is stupid.

01:09:59.550 --> 01:10:00.550
It’s awkward.

01:10:00.550 --> 01:10:01.550
It’s artificial.

01:10:01.550 --> 01:10:02.550
That’s okay.

01:10:02.550 --> 01:10:04.900
Just try it once.”

01:10:04.900 --> 01:10:08.050
So then they'll go and try it, and then they'll
come back and say, “We had an absolutely

01:10:08.050 --> 01:10:09.139
miserable time.

01:10:09.139 --> 01:10:10.139
Really.

01:10:10.139 --> 01:10:11.139
We had a miserable time.

01:10:11.139 --> 01:10:15.150
We couldn't agree on what movie to go to,
and then she took me to her movie and I really

01:10:15.150 --> 01:10:16.739
didn't like it and so we fought all the way
home.

01:10:16.739 --> 01:10:19.050
We’re never doing that again.”

01:10:19.050 --> 01:10:23.349
And I say, well really, you’ve got thirty
years, four hundred days.

01:10:23.349 --> 01:10:24.639
That’s twelve thousand days.

01:10:24.639 --> 01:10:26.380
Okay, so you're not going to do that.

01:10:26.380 --> 01:10:32.619
You’re going to spend the next twelve thousand
days without having any real romantic evenings

01:10:32.619 --> 01:10:34.429
and interactions with your spouse.

01:10:34.429 --> 01:10:35.429
That's your plan.

01:10:35.429 --> 01:10:39.199
And I like doing arithmetic with my clients.

01:10:39.199 --> 01:10:40.519
They hate that.

01:10:40.519 --> 01:10:41.849
They hate arithmetic.

01:10:41.849 --> 01:10:45.460
It’s like well no that sounds like a bad
idea.

01:10:45.460 --> 01:10:49.329
Okay well would you like some romance in your
life or are you just done with that?

01:10:49.329 --> 01:10:54.219
Well really, like people can go for a long
time with no romance at all.

01:10:54.219 --> 01:10:57.150
They say, “Well no maybe, we’d like some
of that.”

01:10:57.150 --> 01:10:58.530
Well how much?

01:10:58.530 --> 01:10:59.690
Once a year.

01:10:59.690 --> 01:11:01.120
Well, no.

01:11:01.120 --> 01:11:02.489
Once a month.

01:11:02.489 --> 01:11:04.550
Well, no.

01:11:04.550 --> 01:11:05.690
Once every two weeks.

01:11:05.690 --> 01:11:07.469
Well, sometimes people are really busy.

01:11:07.469 --> 01:11:10.659
Okay, that beats the hell out of zero.

01:11:10.659 --> 01:11:12.190
Once a week, twice a week.

01:11:12.190 --> 01:11:14.979
Okay, whatever, we’re gonna start with a
range.

01:11:14.979 --> 01:11:17.280
Okay, what would a good evening look like?

01:11:17.280 --> 01:11:22.289
Like if you could both get exactly what you
wanted, what would it look like?

01:11:22.289 --> 01:11:26.949
Then they have to think about that because
the previous theory was, “My stupid partner

01:11:26.949 --> 01:11:30.139
should know what I like” and that's what
the partner is thinking too.

01:11:30.139 --> 01:11:33.940
It’s like good luck with that, no they don't
have a clue especially if they're men.

01:11:33.940 --> 01:11:38.949
They don't have a clue so you have to tell
them what you want and how they could deliver

01:11:38.949 --> 01:11:42.139
it, and vice versa which is very awkward and
horrible.

01:11:42.139 --> 01:11:46.539
And then you have to practice it for six months
because you know, it takes a lot of practice

01:11:46.539 --> 01:11:51.130
to do something sophisticated really well,
and then if you do that it’s like poof,

01:11:51.130 --> 01:11:53.360
you got it for the rest of your life.

01:11:53.360 --> 01:11:58.360
So it’s worth the aggravation, and you know
the first time the rat puts its paw on the

01:11:58.360 --> 01:12:01.699
ladder, its just sort of doing it accidentally.

01:12:01.699 --> 01:12:07.010
You can't expect it to do it well, and that's
exactly how to view your own progress and

01:12:07.010 --> 01:12:08.019
the progress of your partner.

01:12:08.019 --> 01:12:10.840
It’s like let them do it badly for awhile.

01:12:10.840 --> 01:12:12.689
At least they're doing it.

01:12:12.689 --> 01:12:16.530
So if you think they should do it faster,
well look real hard and see how easy it is

01:12:16.530 --> 01:12:20.539
to change your own behavior because it’s
really hard to change your own behavior.

01:12:20.539 --> 01:12:22.900
Everybody says, “I’m going to exercise
three times a week.”

01:12:22.900 --> 01:12:24.699
New Years, and its like no one does.

01:12:24.699 --> 01:12:29.309
They buy the membership and then they feel
guilty for having them, but they don't actually

01:12:29.309 --> 01:12:34.510
go to the gym - and that's a perfect example
of how difficult it is to transform behavior.

01:12:34.510 --> 01:12:39.920
Okay, so back to the diagram here.

01:12:39.920 --> 01:12:42.239
Expectation.

01:12:42.239 --> 01:12:49.289
So, the early models of expectation were cognitive
models and the idea was the rat makes a little

01:12:49.289 --> 01:12:54.719
model of the world, and then he operates in
that world according to what he expects.

01:12:54.719 --> 01:12:56.530
Now there's a problem with that model.

01:12:56.530 --> 01:12:57.530
There's a bunch of them.

01:12:57.530 --> 01:13:01.730
The first is now we know that animals don't
make comprehensive models of the world, and

01:13:01.730 --> 01:13:03.280
that's a huge problem.

01:13:03.280 --> 01:13:05.900
The problem is, well what do they get rid
of, and how?

01:13:05.900 --> 01:13:10.539
But the way you get rid of that problem is
that you introduce motivation into the equation,

01:13:10.539 --> 01:13:15.280
and it’s interesting because the behaviorists
always thought of animals like blank slates

01:13:15.280 --> 01:13:20.670
so they never really took motivation seriously
as a contributor to cognitive modeling.

01:13:20.670 --> 01:13:27.130
It’s also the case that probably ever since
the Enlightenment, people have thought of

01:13:27.130 --> 01:13:30.780
emotion and motivation as forces opposed to
rationality.

01:13:30.780 --> 01:13:35.460
So you know, you're as rational insofar as
you didn't let your emotions and motivations

01:13:35.460 --> 01:13:36.670
influence you.

01:13:36.670 --> 01:13:38.670
But that's a foolish theory.

01:13:38.670 --> 01:13:43.190
There are times when it’s inappropriate
for certain purposes to let certain motivations

01:13:43.190 --> 01:13:49.469
interfere with your observations, but generally
speaking you never think about anything without

01:13:49.469 --> 01:13:51.580
being motivated because why would you?

01:13:51.580 --> 01:13:55.159
Like, there’s a trillion things you can
think of and you're not gonna think of most

01:13:55.159 --> 01:13:56.159
of them.

01:13:56.159 --> 01:13:59.920
You have to have a reason to think of them,
and the reason is that, well, all the time

01:13:59.920 --> 01:14:04.400
you're operating in some sort of motivational
structure, and you can sort of model them

01:14:04.400 --> 01:14:10.050
using the same kind of hierarchy that we use
to model the personality as such.

01:14:10.050 --> 01:14:16.420
I’ll give you an example.

01:14:16.420 --> 01:14:21.139
So let’s think of some basic motivations
and emotions.

01:14:21.139 --> 01:14:25.590
Those aren't good categories by the way because
there's no such things as motivations and

01:14:25.590 --> 01:14:27.860
there's no such thing as emotions.

01:14:27.860 --> 01:14:33.659
The things we loosely categorize into motivations
and emotions each have their own brain circuitry,

01:14:33.659 --> 01:14:37.070
and so there are similarities between them
and there are differences.

01:14:37.070 --> 01:14:38.070
They're not clean categories.

01:14:38.070 --> 01:14:42.619
Well, we’re gonna use the terminology anyways
because we don't have a better one.

01:14:42.619 --> 01:14:50.239
For my purposes, you can roughly define a
motivation as opposed to an emotion as a motivation

01:14:50.239 --> 01:14:55.119
is really something that establishes, you
could say, a goal, but it doesn’t.

01:14:55.119 --> 01:14:56.530
It establishes one of these.

01:14:56.530 --> 01:15:00.949
It doesn't establish a goal, and it doesn't
drive behavior.

01:15:00.949 --> 01:15:03.550
So what it does is let’s say you're hungry
and it’s nighttime, and you want to have

01:15:03.550 --> 01:15:04.550
a peanut butter sandwich.

01:15:04.550 --> 01:15:10.659
So imagine you're sitting there typing away,
and all of a sudden this little image of having

01:15:10.659 --> 01:15:12.340
something to eat creeps into your head.

01:15:12.340 --> 01:15:14.050
It’s not usually quite specific.

01:15:14.050 --> 01:15:16.369
It’s that there's something you would like
to eat.

01:15:16.369 --> 01:15:19.389
So you think, “Ah, I could really use some
popcorn.”

01:15:19.389 --> 01:15:25.440
So you're typing away and the stupid popcorn
thing keeps popping up, and finally your whole

01:15:25.440 --> 01:15:28.889
body is starting to get into the popcorn and
you think “Oh to hell with it, I’ll go

01:15:28.889 --> 01:15:30.229
make some popcorn.”

01:15:30.229 --> 01:15:35.820
So what's happened is that your hypothalamus,
roughly speaking, is monitoring your internal

01:15:35.820 --> 01:15:39.770
body state and it thinks that you need some
- maybe you need some salt, but maybe you

01:15:39.770 --> 01:15:45.090
need some food, and so it’s gonna start
popping up, not a drive.

01:15:45.090 --> 01:15:50.400
It’s not like all of a sudden you’re a
robot, like a popcorn seeking robot and you're

01:15:50.400 --> 01:15:57.030
laying out your chained together condition
popcorn making and eating routines.

01:15:57.030 --> 01:15:59.389
That isn't what happens.

01:15:59.389 --> 01:16:04.939
What happens instead that you start to envision
a new state of being, and that state of being

01:16:04.939 --> 01:16:09.920
has a goal which is you eating some popcorn,
and it has you where you are which is in an

01:16:09.920 --> 01:16:17.699
insufficient state of popcorn and then what's
happening as well is that the motor processes

01:16:17.699 --> 01:16:21.789
that you would use to implement that are also
disinhibited.

01:16:21.789 --> 01:16:22.789
They're there.

01:16:22.789 --> 01:16:24.849
You already know how to do it.

01:16:24.849 --> 01:16:28.469
They get disinhibited or you can think of
them as getting primed.

01:16:28.469 --> 01:16:29.469
They're not.

01:16:29.469 --> 01:16:31.570
They're getting disinhibited, but you can
think of them as getting primed.

01:16:31.570 --> 01:16:37.619
And at some point, that becomes overwhelming
enough - say, pressure from the hypothalamus

01:16:37.619 --> 01:16:39.590
which is really what's bloody well in control.

01:16:39.590 --> 01:16:42.659
It says yeah, yeah, enough internet surfing.

01:16:42.659 --> 01:16:46.249
Off to the popcorn, and so you leap up and
then what do you do?

01:16:46.249 --> 01:16:49.219
You reorient the way you're looking at the
world.

01:16:49.219 --> 01:16:54.360
Everything in the world becomes low resolution,
or even zero except those few things that

01:16:54.360 --> 01:16:56.739
are relevant to your new quest.

01:16:56.739 --> 01:16:57.949
Down the stairs you go.

01:16:57.949 --> 01:17:02.710
You find the right pan; you find the popcorn,
getting irritated if you can't find it, right,

01:17:02.710 --> 01:17:08.170
because - now you see it’s interesting because
your emotions align along your goals.

01:17:08.170 --> 01:17:13.809
As long as you're pursuing a beeline to your
goal, and everything's laying itself out not

01:17:13.809 --> 01:17:19.900
as you’d expect, but as you want, then your
emotions stay positive but as soon as something

01:17:19.900 --> 01:17:24.429
deviates then you're irritated, and the reason
for that is like let’s say you can't find

01:17:24.429 --> 01:17:25.679
a popcorn pan.

01:17:25.679 --> 01:17:29.449
Well your brain is computing, well something
like this.

01:17:29.449 --> 01:17:36.090
This is obviously not how it works, but it’s
an - x amount of energy and matter from popcorn

01:17:36.090 --> 01:17:37.989
means it has x value.

01:17:37.989 --> 01:17:45.469
If you expend a whole bunch of energy running
around in your clueless state trying to acquire

01:17:45.469 --> 01:17:48.969
popcorn, you can easily get to the point where
you've expended more energy and time than

01:17:48.969 --> 01:17:50.650
the popcorn delivers.

01:17:50.650 --> 01:17:53.150
And if you do that for a month, then you die.

01:17:53.150 --> 01:17:58.880
So it’s perfectly reasonable for you to
get irritated when the path length to your

01:17:58.880 --> 01:18:03.739
destination increases radically, and you might
get even more irritated if you can’t find

01:18:03.739 --> 01:18:08.920
any popcorn, and it’s like oh no I have
to dispense with the popcorn micro personality

01:18:08.920 --> 01:18:11.860
and it’s all irritated because it’s sort
of alive.

01:18:11.860 --> 01:18:15.380
It’s irritated you have to squash it, and
then you have to come up with well maybe I

01:18:15.380 --> 01:18:16.829
can have a peanut butter sandwich.

01:18:16.829 --> 01:18:22.820
And then pop, up comes a new micro personality
that orients itself towards the goal, and

01:18:22.820 --> 01:18:26.829
so all the time you’re doing that, you're
always in a motivated state.

01:18:26.829 --> 01:18:33.800
Now, it’s not a drive because it’s not
deterministic, but it does have a behavioral

01:18:33.800 --> 01:18:34.800
element.

01:18:34.800 --> 01:18:37.540
You have all these Piagetian subroutines.

01:18:37.540 --> 01:18:43.289
They're stored in your body, you know, all
your body capacity or your skills.

01:18:43.289 --> 01:18:48.250
And you know which set of those you’re likely
to use if you’re going to do x thing.

01:18:48.250 --> 01:18:53.989
So those get prepared, and then your perceptions
reorient towards the new goal.

01:18:53.989 --> 01:18:55.729
It’s like a hunting goal.

01:18:55.729 --> 01:18:59.650
You're fixated on it, and then your emotions
orient along that so as long as things are

01:18:59.650 --> 01:19:01.570
going well, you're happy.

01:19:01.570 --> 01:19:02.699
You’re hopeful.

01:19:02.699 --> 01:19:03.699
Things are proceeding.

01:19:03.699 --> 01:19:07.769
That’s positive emotion; that’s dopaminergically
mediated, and if things aren't going well

01:19:07.769 --> 01:19:12.040
it’s either, well, it’s any amount of
negative emotion from mild irritation to full

01:19:12.040 --> 01:19:13.800
blown temper tantrum.

01:19:13.800 --> 01:19:19.689
So that’s - and you’re - okay that’s
the simple story.

01:19:19.689 --> 01:19:25.409
The complex story is okay, you’re hungry,
you’re thirsty, you’re tired, you’re

01:19:25.409 --> 01:19:27.079
hot, you’re lonely.

01:19:27.079 --> 01:19:28.079
You need to play.

01:19:28.079 --> 01:19:32.690
Okay, so let's say you've got, for the sake
of argument, let’s say you have eight fundamental

01:19:32.690 --> 01:19:34.789
motivational systems.

01:19:34.789 --> 01:19:36.699
Now, who cares how many.

01:19:36.699 --> 01:19:37.699
It’s a finite set.

01:19:37.699 --> 01:19:38.869
It’s under twenty.

01:19:38.869 --> 01:19:40.599
Depends on whether you include anger.

01:19:40.599 --> 01:19:42.380
Is anger motivation or emotion?

01:19:42.380 --> 01:19:44.280
Is pain motivation or emotion?

01:19:44.280 --> 01:19:48.530
We don’t care because they're not formal
categories.

01:19:48.530 --> 01:19:57.269
So anyways, there's a set number of them and
then so they have to run to satiation.

01:19:57.269 --> 01:20:02.010
So what satiation means is they set up an
end state; you perform the routines, you achieve

01:20:02.010 --> 01:20:04.550
the end state, and the system shuts down.

01:20:04.550 --> 01:20:05.840
It’s satiated.

01:20:05.840 --> 01:20:10.610
Now you'll experience that as satisfaction,
not as pleasure.

01:20:10.610 --> 01:20:12.829
And you’ll think well what's the difference?

01:20:12.829 --> 01:20:14.579
It’s tight.

01:20:14.579 --> 01:20:18.590
It’s complicated, but it’s important.

01:20:18.590 --> 01:20:24.539
The kind of positive emotion that people really
like is associated with search and the hope,

01:20:24.539 --> 01:20:29.159
not with the acquiry because what happens
when you acquire is well you're no longer

01:20:29.159 --> 01:20:36.059
in this state which has an impulse to action
in it, but that's exciting.

01:20:36.059 --> 01:20:37.059
That’s fun.

01:20:37.059 --> 01:20:38.059
You're on the track of something.

01:20:38.059 --> 01:20:40.550
It’s like you’re alive while you're searching
for something.

01:20:40.550 --> 01:20:41.550
Well then you find it.

01:20:41.550 --> 01:20:44.999
It’s like good, you need to find it, but
then the whole thing disappears and you're

01:20:44.999 --> 01:20:48.040
sitting there thinking well I’ve just graduated.

01:20:48.040 --> 01:20:49.040
Now what?

01:20:49.040 --> 01:20:51.090
Right, because that’s what’ll happen when
you graduate.

01:20:51.090 --> 01:20:52.669
It’s like hey you're a student.

01:20:52.669 --> 01:20:54.230
You've got status.

01:20:54.230 --> 01:20:57.219
You’ve got a hierarchy of values.

01:20:57.219 --> 01:21:01.860
You've got a way of orienting yourself in
the world, and you have an identity.

01:21:01.860 --> 01:21:05.130
So you're in there, and everyone's happy about
it and so one day you graduate.

01:21:05.130 --> 01:21:06.659
It’s like success.

01:21:06.659 --> 01:21:11.249
Well really, it’s like poof that’s gone,
and the next thing you know, you’re like

01:21:11.249 --> 01:21:13.800
handing out coffee at the local Starbucks.

01:21:13.800 --> 01:21:16.080
Yeah, well you get the point.

01:21:16.080 --> 01:21:17.369
You've experienced this all the time in your
life.

01:21:17.369 --> 01:21:18.949
You hit a pinnacle.

01:21:18.949 --> 01:21:24.650
You've obtained something, but the problem
is that now what?

01:21:24.650 --> 01:21:27.119
You have to conjure up a whole new you.

01:21:27.119 --> 01:21:31.599
So some of it is satisfying, you’ve accomplished
a goal, but some of it’s just a big pain

01:21:31.599 --> 01:21:32.599
in the neck.

01:21:32.599 --> 01:21:38.300
It’s like uh oh, student me has disappeared
and now it’s frantically searching for employment

01:21:38.300 --> 01:21:44.869
me, and that me doesn't have the status, and
is in a space of much more radical uncertainty.

01:21:44.869 --> 01:21:52.449
So don't be thinking that getting to where
you want to go is what you want.

01:21:52.449 --> 01:21:56.179
What you want is to bloody well make sure
that the voyage is worth it.

01:21:56.179 --> 01:22:02.679
Now it’s very interesting because people
are really wired up this way, and I think

01:22:02.679 --> 01:22:05.820
it’s because we’re so radically exploratory.

01:22:05.820 --> 01:22:14.269
So, when you’re proceeding towards a valued
goal, let’s call that a cue that goal attainment

01:22:14.269 --> 01:22:15.659
is about to occur.

01:22:15.659 --> 01:22:17.510
It’s going to occur in some chain.

01:22:17.510 --> 01:22:21.260
A leads to B, B leads to C, C leads to D,
and so on.

01:22:21.260 --> 01:22:23.699
You're like a hound on a trail.

01:22:23.699 --> 01:22:25.659
It’s positive, like yeah this is working.

01:22:25.659 --> 01:22:26.659
Yeah, this is working.

01:22:26.659 --> 01:22:27.659
Yeah, this is working.

01:22:27.659 --> 01:22:28.659
That’s positive.

01:22:28.659 --> 01:22:32.110
It’s moving you ahead, and that's really
where you experience a lot of your positive

01:22:32.110 --> 01:22:33.110
emotion.

01:22:33.110 --> 01:22:34.110
That’s dopaminergically mediated.

01:22:34.110 --> 01:22:38.989
That’s the system you kick with cocaine
or any of the other drugs of abuse that people

01:22:38.989 --> 01:22:42.050
use that don't quell anxiety.

01:22:42.050 --> 01:22:46.489
It’s a very, very desirable motivational
state.

01:22:46.489 --> 01:22:54.039
I read awhile back somebody who said you can
buy anything but you can't buy wanting anything,

01:22:54.039 --> 01:22:59.909
and that's a staggeringly interesting comment
because you think well, is wanting a positive

01:22:59.909 --> 01:23:00.909
state?

01:23:00.909 --> 01:23:05.159
Well you don't have it, so that's kind of
annoying and you think, “No I don't want

01:23:05.159 --> 01:23:06.159
to want things.

01:23:06.159 --> 01:23:07.159
I want to have them.”

01:23:07.159 --> 01:23:10.250
It’s like yeah, don’t be so sure about
that.

01:23:10.250 --> 01:23:13.530
So one of the things, one of the ways people
have figured out how to deal with that is

01:23:13.530 --> 01:23:18.340
they decide they want something that they
can't get and that's actually extraordinarily

01:23:18.340 --> 01:23:20.429
smart, and it’s a deep archetypal idea.

01:23:20.429 --> 01:23:21.670
Here’s an example.

01:23:21.670 --> 01:23:24.340
You’ve seen Pinocchio, right?

01:23:24.340 --> 01:23:27.620
Okay, so Pinocchio is about a puppet, a marionette.

01:23:27.620 --> 01:23:30.510
So that means someone else is pulling his
strings.

01:23:30.510 --> 01:23:31.510
Right?

01:23:31.510 --> 01:23:32.559
We don't know who.

01:23:32.559 --> 01:23:34.919
Sometimes it’s virtually Satan himself.

01:23:34.919 --> 01:23:37.659
Remember the Stromboli?

01:23:37.659 --> 01:23:40.989
There's another character just after him is
the coachman.

01:23:40.989 --> 01:23:46.829
The coachman, he’s like king of the thieves
and he ends up pulling Pinocchio’s strings.

01:23:46.829 --> 01:23:52.510
So there’s one scene in the Disney movie
where the coachman reveals himself and he’s

01:23:52.510 --> 01:23:57.409
completely red, he has really big teeth; he’s
a satanic figure, and he scares the fox and

01:23:57.409 --> 01:24:01.719
the cat who are like low level criminals who
think they're bad, but you know, have never

01:24:01.719 --> 01:24:02.719
really met bad.

01:24:02.719 --> 01:24:07.289
He just scares them right into the corner,
and so it’s all of a sudden this person

01:24:07.289 --> 01:24:11.840
reveals the archetype behind and just closes
back down pulling Pinocchio’s strings.

01:24:11.840 --> 01:24:12.999
Okay, now.

01:24:12.999 --> 01:24:14.550
He’s a marionette.

01:24:14.550 --> 01:24:18.969
Well, the first question that arises is who’s
pulling your strings?

01:24:18.969 --> 01:24:20.860
And that’s a really good question.

01:24:20.860 --> 01:24:25.329
One of the things Jung said was “People
don't have ideas, ideas have people.”

01:24:25.329 --> 01:24:30.229
You know, you can think about that for about
three months and it should make you afraid

01:24:30.229 --> 01:24:37.019
the whole time because as soon as you get
that, you think uh oh.

01:24:37.019 --> 01:24:42.210
Let’s say you're an ideologue of some sort
because some of you probably are: socialist,

01:24:42.210 --> 01:24:45.810
fascist, environmentalist, something that
has -ist on the end.

01:24:45.810 --> 01:24:48.679
It’s like you think those are your ideas.

01:24:48.679 --> 01:24:50.519
They’re not.

01:24:50.519 --> 01:24:52.900
You're their tools and that's fine.

01:24:52.900 --> 01:24:58.039
If you want to be a tool of those ideas, you
go right ahead, but you don't know where those

01:24:58.039 --> 01:25:01.749
ideas are leading and they could well be leading
somewhere you do not want to go.

01:25:01.749 --> 01:25:06.590
Now the plus side is you don't have to take
responsibility for it because you’re just

01:25:06.590 --> 01:25:08.590
drifting in an ocean of thought.

01:25:08.590 --> 01:25:12.650
But one of the things that I learned from
Jung was you should figure out just exactly

01:25:12.650 --> 01:25:17.550
which ideas are possessing you and then you
should decide if those are your ideas, and

01:25:17.550 --> 01:25:19.460
the probability that they are is zero.

01:25:19.460 --> 01:25:21.480
They’re not your ideas.

01:25:21.480 --> 01:25:23.449
It’s hard to have your own ideas.

01:25:23.449 --> 01:25:29.039
You have to derive them from your own experience,
and especially when you're not very old, you

01:25:29.039 --> 01:25:34.729
don't have that much experience and so you
can grab books full of ideas and claim them

01:25:34.729 --> 01:25:37.619
for your own which is not acceptable.

01:25:37.619 --> 01:25:42.969
You can use those ideas to inform your ideas,
but they're not your ideas.

01:25:42.969 --> 01:25:47.539
You're just a puppet, and you know the Pinocchio
movie is very interesting that way because

01:25:47.539 --> 01:25:51.090
he's a puppet of forces that are really not
on his side.

01:25:51.090 --> 01:25:53.080
Seriously not on his side.

01:25:53.080 --> 01:25:55.739
Okay anyways, back to the Pinocchio movie.

01:25:55.739 --> 01:25:57.059
Give me one second.

01:25:57.059 --> 01:25:58.729
So Geppetto doesn't have a son.

01:25:58.729 --> 01:26:00.130
Now, Geppetto’s a good guy right?

01:26:00.130 --> 01:26:01.150
He’s a toymaker.

01:26:01.150 --> 01:26:03.179
He’s got this little warm cabin.

01:26:03.179 --> 01:26:04.179
It’s cozy.

01:26:04.179 --> 01:26:05.179
He’s a good guy.

01:26:05.179 --> 01:26:06.179
He’s friendly.

01:26:06.179 --> 01:26:07.179
He has a little cat.

01:26:07.179 --> 01:26:10.139
He pays attention to his little fish, he takes
care of that.

01:26:10.139 --> 01:26:13.999
So we know Geppetto’s a good guy and he
makes this puppet and he thinks, “Man I’ve

01:26:13.999 --> 01:26:16.959
got a puppet but maybe I’d rather have something
real.

01:26:16.959 --> 01:26:18.689
That’s a good question for parents.

01:26:18.689 --> 01:26:21.770
You want a puppet, or do you want something
real?

01:26:21.770 --> 01:26:26.250
And the answer is, generally often, I’ll
just have a puppet as long as it does exactly

01:26:26.250 --> 01:26:30.489
what it - maybe it’ll be a medical school
puppet because that's certainly one good form

01:26:30.489 --> 01:26:32.030
of puppet.

01:26:32.030 --> 01:26:37.659
Okay, so Geppetto decides cause he’s a good
guy, he thinks no no I don't want this thing

01:26:37.659 --> 01:26:38.659
to have strings.

01:26:38.659 --> 01:26:39.659
I want it to be its own thing.

01:26:39.659 --> 01:26:41.960
I want it to be autonomous, individual, real.

01:26:41.960 --> 01:26:44.199
Okay, so what does he do?

01:26:44.199 --> 01:26:45.199
He wishes.

01:26:45.199 --> 01:26:46.199
Right?

01:26:46.199 --> 01:26:47.199
What’s he wish on?

01:26:47.199 --> 01:26:48.199
A star.

01:26:48.199 --> 01:26:50.019
Why do people wish on stars?

01:26:50.019 --> 01:26:51.599
Of all the crazy things.

01:26:51.599 --> 01:26:54.829
Well it’s a divine thing.

01:26:54.829 --> 01:26:59.469
It’s like there’s heaven, it’s all - it’s
at the infinite.

01:26:59.469 --> 01:27:02.449
You go out at night and you see the stars,
which you can't in the city but you go out

01:27:02.449 --> 01:27:06.940
somewhere dark and you see the stars, and
it’s like you and the infinite.

01:27:06.940 --> 01:27:08.159
And it really is.

01:27:08.159 --> 01:27:13.889
It’s a perfect representation of your relationship
with the infinite because the universe is

01:27:13.889 --> 01:27:17.949
about as infinite as you need, and it’s
going to give you a sense of awe and that's

01:27:17.949 --> 01:27:21.199
partly why archaic people partly thought God
was in the sky.

01:27:21.199 --> 01:27:24.809
It’s like, they weren't stupid, these people.

01:27:24.809 --> 01:27:32.389
They thought God is what fills you with awe,
so you look at the sky and think wow, that’s

01:27:32.389 --> 01:27:33.389
something.

01:27:33.389 --> 01:27:35.829
It’s an instinct.

01:27:35.829 --> 01:27:38.800
It’s not a cognitive construction.

01:27:38.800 --> 01:27:42.619
So anyways, you aim at a star, and it’s
this twinkling thing in the darkness, right.

01:27:42.619 --> 01:27:49.230
It’s the light in the darkness and it’s
above the worldly environment.

01:27:49.230 --> 01:27:51.880
It’s something transcendent.

01:27:51.880 --> 01:27:53.909
So that’s what Geppetto wishes on.

01:27:53.909 --> 01:27:56.110
It’s like, I’m gonna aim high.

01:27:56.110 --> 01:27:57.249
That’s what he says.

01:27:57.249 --> 01:27:58.249
Aim high.

01:27:58.249 --> 01:28:01.349
And he thinks well it’s not very likely
but I might as well aim high.

01:28:01.349 --> 01:28:04.329
That’s a very good piece of advice.

01:28:04.329 --> 01:28:08.070
You want to aim so high you can't attain it
because then you've got something worth - you

01:28:08.070 --> 01:28:09.650
might think that’s such perverse.

01:28:09.650 --> 01:28:12.429
That’s such a perverse piece of advice.

01:28:12.429 --> 01:28:15.579
Why would you chase something you can't have?

01:28:15.579 --> 01:28:21.610
Well the answer might be cause the chasing
is worth it, and then you might think well

01:28:21.610 --> 01:28:22.610
what’s worth it?

01:28:22.610 --> 01:28:24.280
Well I can give you a hint about that too.

01:28:24.280 --> 01:28:29.070
It’s like develop your character because
what you've got is what you carry with you

01:28:29.070 --> 01:28:34.269
wherever you go, and it’s way better to
be able to do things than to be rich cause

